  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Pre-Assessment",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Pre-Assessment",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Talking to Machines",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Who Decides?",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I use AI effectively?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how AI generates responses by recognizing patterns and predicting likely outcomes. Through a “Yes, and…” game, they simulate AI’s response-building process. They experiment using text and images to craft effective prompts using AI Chat levels. By the end, they understand how AI processes information and how to use strategic prompting for better results.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I use AI effectively?**\n\nExplore how AI predicts responses and practice crafting better prompts to get more useful AI-generated answers."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Talking to Machines",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: The AI's Brain",
      "name": "Lesson 2: The AI's Brain",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do AI models learn?**\n\nIn this lesson, students act as neurons in an unplugged neural network, building sentences one word at a time to experience how an AI predicts text from limited and full context. They then train their own AI model to recognize objects in the ocean and discover that the training data they provide determines what their AI can and cannot recognize — especially when they train it on a word that has no single right answer. The lesson closes by connecting that hands-on experience to the large language models behind the chatbots students already use.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Unplugged Neural Networks Activity Guide (one for each group of 3 to 5 students)",
        "student_overview": "**How do AI models learn?**\n\nIn this lesson, you act as a neuron in a neural network, then train your own AI to recognize what belongs in the ocean. Along the way, you find out that what an AI learns depends entirely on what you teach it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: The AI's Brain",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 4: Smart or Just Predictable?",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Hallucinations and Contradictions",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Why does AI make things up — and can we stop it?**\n\nStudents discover that an AI chatbot states false information as confidently as true information, then experience how adding context changes its answers. They take control of what a chatbot knows by curating the information it draws from, finding that a bot with bad context is unreliable even though it has context. The lesson builds toward the understanding that context reduces hallucinations but does not remove the need to verify AI output.",
        "student_overview": "**Why does AI make things up — and can we stop it?**\n\nCatch an AI chatbot making things up, then see what happens when you give it a document to work from. Take control of what a chatbot knows and find out whether better information is enough to make it trustworthy."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 4: Smart or Just Predictable?",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 11: Ownership, Ethics, and Creativity",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Ownership, Ethics, and Creativity",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What are the ethical considerations of AI collaboration?**\n\nStudents explore the ethical tradeoffs of using AI in creative contexts by engaging with real-world controversies involving ownership, attribution, and compensation. Through a values-based warm-up, rotating discussion stations, and a reflection on guiding principles, students consider how AI-generated content affects creators and what fairness looks like in these scenarios. The lesson helps students evaluate AI’s role as a collaborator and justify when its use may or may not be appropriate.",
        "preparation": "- Review the Ownership and AI Stations Resource to see how to set up your room for the Stations activity in the lesson\n- Print copies of the Station Instructions from the Ownership and AI Stations resource.\n- Print copies of the Ownership and AI Stations activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**What are the ethical considerations of AI collaboration?**\n\nIn this lesson, you explore real-world examples of how AI is used to create things like art, music, and writing — and the ethical questions that come with it. You think about values like fairness, credit, and consent, and decide what matters most when AI uses other people’s work."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 11: Ownership, Ethics, and Creativity",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 6: Understanding Bias",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Understanding Bias",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What is AI bias, and how does it emerge?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how AI bias emerges from unrepresentative training data and examine biased responses from AI Chat. In the main activity, students take on a decision-making role — hospital triage nurse, college admissions officer, or hiring manager — compare their own selections to AI's, and refine prompts to see how training data and system prompts shape AI's choices. Throughout the lesson, students discuss strategies for reducing bias and aligning AI systems with human values.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the AI Audit activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**What is AI bias, and how does it emerge?**\n\nExplore how AI bias emerges from training data by analyzing AI Chat's responses, then take on a decision-making role and compare your choices to AI's. Discuss ways to make AI fairer and less biased."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 6: Understanding Bias",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 7: AI's Wild Imagination",
      "name": "Lesson 6: AI's Wild Imagination",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What are some examples of AI hallucinations? What are their impacts?**\n\nThis lesson explores the real-world impacts of AI hallucinations. Building on their previous understanding of AI errors, students analyze real-world case studies where AI misinformation has caused harm. Through discussion, case studies, and fact-checking strategies, students evaluate AI’s reliability and consider its ethical implications in fields like law, healthcare, and journalism.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Hallucination Case Studies activity guide (one per student)\n- Print copies of the Jigsaw Articles (one copy group of four students)",
        "student_overview": "**What are some examples of AI hallucinations? What are their impacts?**\n\nAI can generate false information that has real consequences. In this lesson, you explore examples of AI hallucinations in the real world and practice fact-checking strategies to determine when AI’s responses can and cannot be trusted."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 7: AI's Wild Imagination",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 8: Debugging and Refining Outputs",
      "name": "Lesson 7: Debugging and Refining Outputs",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Can we improve AI's responses?**\n\nStudents discover how small prompt details change AI output through an unplugged partner activity, then apply the TaCoFoRT framework to build and test their own AI chatbot agent for a business of their choice. After testing their chatbot with three types of questions, students debug and revise their system prompt or Retrieval information based on what they find. The lesson closes with students summarizing what they learned about improving AI responses on a classroom Anchor Chart.",
        "preparation": "- Provide copies of the Prompt Showdown activity guide (one per pair)\n- Provide copies of the Career Spec Sheet handout (one per student)\n- [Optional] Prepare a classroom anchor chart for an optional wrap up activity containing five columns - Task, Context, Format, Retrieval, and Tone.",
        "student_overview": "**Can we improve AI's responses?**\n\nYou compare how small changes in format and tone affect an AI's response, then use the TaCoFoRT framework to build and test an AI chatbot agent for a business of your choice. After testing your chatbot, you debug and improve it based on what you find."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 8: Debugging and Refining Outputs",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 10: Remixing, Creativity, and Originality",
      "name": "Lesson 8: Remixing, Creativity, and Originality",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "background": "light",
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Is AI-generated content truly creative — and who owns it?**\n\nStudents explore AI Music Lab, then compose a short theme for a movie scene or game moment using AI-generated drums and sampled tracks. After a video on music production careers, students take on industry perspectives — musician, remixer, streaming executive, and music lawyer — in table debates over whether AI should be used to create music and art. The lesson closes with students committing their own positions on the creativity and ownership of AI-generated content.",
        "preparation": "- Print and cut apart one set of Debate Perspective Cards for each group of four students. Each sheet contains all four cards — print single-sided so students can write their arguments on the back.",
        "student_overview": "**Is AI-generated content truly creative — and who owns it?**\n\nCompose your own theme using AI drums and sampled tracks in Music Lab — then debate whether AI should be making music at all, and who owns it when it does."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 10: Remixing, Creativity, and Originality",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 12: AI's Role in Society",
      "name": "Lesson 9: AI's Role in Society",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How should AI be used in solving real-world problems?**\n\nStudents choose a real-world example of AI in use within their community — grocery stores, medical diagnosis, public transportation, schools, or the environment — and investigate who benefits and who might be harmed by its implementation, using AI Chat to summarize and fact-check across article types. They express their findings and point of view on a vision board capturing their hopes, fears, questions, and role in AI's future, then collaborate with AI to distill the board into a power phrase they share with peers.",
        "student_overview": "**How should AI be used in solving real-world problems?**\n\nYou'll pick a real example of AI at work in your community and dig into it with an AI research partner: Who does it help? Who could it hurt? What's the real story behind the headlines? Then you'll turn what you find into a vision board — your hopes, your fears, your questions, and the role you want to play in AI's future. Finally, you'll team up with AI to shrink your whole board down to one powerful phrase — and share it with your classmates."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 12: AI's Role in Society",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: Beyond Words",
      "name": "[REMOVED] Beyond Words",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How does AI process different types of media?**\n\n In this lesson, students explore how multimodal AI models process and interpret different types of media by comparing AI-generated responses to text-only vs text and image inputs. Through hands-on activities and discussions, they analyze AI’s capabilities, limitations, and ethical considerations, reinforcing their understanding of effective prompting and privacy risks in AI applications.",
        "student_overview": "**How does AI process different types of media?**\n\nIn this lesson, explore how multimodal AI models interpret different types of media by comparing responses to text-only vs. text and image inputs. Through hands-on activities and discussions, discover what AI can and can’t do, how to ask better questions, and why privacy matters when using AI."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: Beyond Words",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 5: Uncovering Contradictions",
      "name": "[REMOVED] Uncovering Contradictions",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Can AI contradict itself?**\n\nStudents will be asked to explore the reasons why AI might provide contradictory answers. They will practice refining prompts in order to both induce and eliminate contradictions in AI-generated responses. Additionally, they will examine ways in which AI contradictions can have real-world effects.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Examine AI Contradictions activity guide (one for each pair of students)",
        "student_overview": "**Can AI contradict itself?**\n\nThere are times when even the most sophisticated AI can provide two or more answers that seemingly contradict. In this scenario, it is important to know why this might occur and to employ a handful of strategies to help overcome it. This lesson will provide you the tools to refine prompts in order to reduce or eliminate contradictory responses."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 5: Uncovering Contradictions",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 9: AI as a Co-Creator",
      "name": "[REMOVED] Lesson 9: AI as a Co-Creator",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can AI enhance creativity?**\n\nIn this lesson, students take on creative roles to design and launch a product or app that helps students succeed in school. Working in teams, they collaborate with AI across four stages—research, brainstorming, content generation, and editing—to co-create campaign materials like slogans, scripts, and visuals. The activity emphasizes decomposition, prompting strategy, and human-AI collaboration, helping students develop both technical and creative problem-solving skills.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Collaborate on a Product Launch activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can AI enhance creativity?**\n\nIn this lesson, you’ll work with a team to help launch a brand-new product or app for students. You’ll use AI to research, brainstorm, and build your part of the campaign—like coming up with a name, writing a script, or designing a logo. You’ll learn how to guide the AI, improve its responses, and use your own creativity to make something original."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 9: AI as a Co-Creator",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 13: AI Time Capsule Project",
      "name": "Lesson 10: Product Launch Project",
      "absolute_position": 14,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this project, students invent a fictional product that solves a real problem from their own lives and take it through a launch cycle: Define, Build, Test, and Ship. They document their product in a factual spec sheet, build and test a customer-facing chatbot powered by a system prompt they compose, and launch with a social media post assembled from AI-generated assets they keep, modify, or reject. Students use AI at every stage, but the project centers their judgment — verifying claims against facts, diagnosing chatbot errors, and deciding when AI output isn't good enough to ship.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Product Launch Project Planning Guide (one per student).\n- Decide how you'll form teams, or plan to let students choose",
        "student_overview": "In this project, you invent a product that solves a real problem from your own life, then launch it: a factual spec sheet, a customer chatbot you build and test yourself, and a social media post made with AI assets you chose to keep, change, or reject. You'll use AI at every stage, but you're in control."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 13: AI Time Capsule Project",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-5",
        "script.name": "problem-solving-with-ai-2026-dev"
      }
    },
