  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "f6284d8b-7dca-4780-9dd5-205dcc8b14e9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6284d8b-7dca-4780-9dd5-205dcc8b14e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9d3d6f67-1131-48ec-840c-b477292397fc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fc587c0-ec32-4e60-b9b5-a868c54d843d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the two music streaming websites. Give students a moment to examine both and to decide which is the more modern version. Take a vote by show of hands.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fc587c0-ec32-4e60-b9b5-a868c54d843d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d4d3e030-a49d-40c3-97a4-591aff3c443b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "29c25260-dff0-4624-9617-978686f278b1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Say**: Both of these pages were made by an AI in about twenty seconds each. Both times, the AI was asked same thing: Generate a homepage for a music streaming service. The only word that was changed was the year. \n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "29c25260-dff0-4624-9617-978686f278b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d4d3e030-a49d-40c3-97a4-591aff3c443b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cc61bd77-3f2d-4bdf-b638-36ddd29c6d2f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What details did the AI decide to include beyond what was written in the prompt?\nTurn and Talk — one minute in pairs. \nPopcorn Share: volunteers pop in with one observation each until the ideas thin out.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the prompt specified only three things and that the AI decided everything else — not only how the page looks, but what the page is. It invented a company name, a navigation bar, a section heading, a layout, and album titles. Students should further notice that both pages share nearly all of those decisions. Both are called StreamTunes. Both say \"Featured Today.\" Both are organized around browsing album art. Changing the year changed the styling. It did not change the AI's idea of what a music homepage is. organized around browsing album art. Changing the year changed the styling. It did not change the AI's idea of what a music homepage is.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cc61bd77-3f2d-4bdf-b638-36ddd29c6d2f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d4d3e030-a49d-40c3-97a4-591aff3c443b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5aceb0c-52a7-482e-9b1b-0028490ac33c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Lesson Objectives\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5aceb0c-52a7-482e-9b1b-0028490ac33c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d4d3e030-a49d-40c3-97a4-591aff3c443b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5af1c0b-eca5-4264-9555-a9cbf1233002",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will experiment and analyze how prompts affect AI output as they work to build a web page. They will observe how AI's output does not always match intent, which requires prompt refinement.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of 2-3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the \"Who Decides?\" activity guide.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Create with AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5af1c0b-eca5-4264-9555-a9cbf1233002",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7ad5ada-c8df-4ace-bfdf-5f3f0b03bb44",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Say**: You are going to do a creative exercise with a partner in which you will select one of three different websites to create with the help of AI. You will use AI to assist with brainstorming ideas, creating the website's front page, generating an image to include, and styling the page. You will have twenty minutes to complete your site, so don't feel pressure to create something perfect. \n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7ad5ada-c8df-4ace-bfdf-5f3f0b03bb44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a081d635-b049-4d74-913a-7fffd50910be",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**\nNavigate to Lesson 1, Level 1 in Code Studio. This is an AI Chat level in which students will select one of three possible websites to create : \na retro food delivery page that might have existed 30 years ago (near the dawn of the internet age)\na social media page that might have been used by the 13 colonies, if the internet had been invented back then\na ride sharing page that might exist 50 years in the future\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students use the AI Chat to brainstorm suggestions about what to include on the website that they have chosen to pursue. They should write down the top three suggestions in their activity guides.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for how students narrow their list — the interesting decision is what they leave out. When a student is choosing, ask: \"Which one of these would change the page the most?\" Students writing down general ideas will get less out of the subsequent levels than students writing down specific ones. Don't correct them. Note who they are, because the difference shows up on screen in ten minutes.\n",
        "progression_name": "Brainstorm Website Ideas"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a081d635-b049-4d74-913a-7fffd50910be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df1f3ac5-5b23-42fc-b148-7f5f8ca2ab78",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This is a choice level, so students only need to select and complete one of the levels. There are many choice levels throughout the course that will encourage students to choose the option that is most interesting to them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df1f3ac5-5b23-42fc-b148-7f5f8ca2ab78",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adec2321-5ff4-403b-ae97-2ba9e220d676",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Say**: Now that you have some ideas for what to include on your web page, you will use the AI Tutor within Web Lab to create it quickly. Use the tabs on the left-side of the screen to navigate between the instructions and the AI Tutor. Explore the tool and the interface with your partner and let's see what you can create! \n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "adec2321-5ff4-403b-ae97-2ba9e220d676",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f292ff9-b0f1-41af-877d-277228a5b6e8",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Say**: The information you give an AI is called a prompt. A prompt may include words, images, examples, or instructions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nDefine prompt\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f292ff9-b0f1-41af-877d-277228a5b6e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "021da97e-a3dc-4a79-9b76-99a34a48a865",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**\nDirect students to Lesson 1, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4 of this activity.\n* Level 2: Students use the AI Tutor within Web Lab to create the initial page for their website. They incrementally add the suggestions that they gathered from the previous level into AI Tutor to revise their website one step at a time.\n* Level 3: Students use image generation to create the image that users will first see at the top of the site.\n* Level 4: Students will return to Web Lab to add in the image that they generated in Level 3. They will also ask AI Tutor to restyle the page to match look and feel of the image that they attached.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** \nEncourage students to move quickly through the levels; achieving perfection is not the goal. \n* On Level 2, remind students to use the tabs on the left side to navigate between the instructions and the AI Tutor page. The will write their prompts in the dialogue box that appear in AI Tutor. Students might get hung up on the Accept / Reject prompt that pops up when they put in a prompt. Here they can choose to get rid of AI's suggestions or accept them and update the page accordingly. When accepting the changes, students must write what they changed in the green dialogue box in order to save the change and update the page. \n* On Level 3, students often pause over how to describe an image in words. Name that pause as the work: \"What would you have to say to get something that looks older or more futuristic?\" \n* On Level 4, make sure that students attach the image that they downloaded in level 3. They can click Add File or copy/paste the image into AI Tutor. When styling, listen for surprise at colors nobody typed — ask: \"Where did the AI get those?\" If two students chose the same site, ask: \"You started from the same place. Why do your pages look different?\"\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Build a Website with AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "021da97e-a3dc-4a79-9b76-99a34a48a865",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af175a2d-6254-4692-bfe6-473e666796dd",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** \nStudents will examine others' sites and look for patterns that recur across web pages.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nStudents will participate in a Gallery Walk in which they circulate and view classmates' web sites. Students should look for similar patterns see across websites. They will describe the pattern and keep track of how many times you see it in their activity guide using hash marks.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Website Gallery Walk "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af175a2d-6254-4692-bfe6-473e666796dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ca74888-3346-47c4-89cb-ef72fb871db4",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Navigate to Lesson 1, Level 5 on Code Studio. This is a Free Response level in which students will type in a response and submit it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nHave students input the most common patterns that they saw across websites and submit. You can share the results from the students in real-time which will lead nicely into the subsequent discussion.\n",
        "progression_name": "Pattern Recognition"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ca74888-3346-47c4-89cb-ef72fb871db4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ee80079-2f7f-405f-b0af-d37b204b1265",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you are using a projector to display this level, you can display student student responses as they are submitted to help spark a class discussion and to see, at a glance, where patterns are converging across websites. Some possible patterns to explore:\n* Most of the websites will generate an invented brand name\n* Navigation bar with similar items across the top\n* A tagline under the name. Often starts with \"Your.\" In testing, four out of four pages did.\n* A copyright line at the bottom. Usually with a year and the phrase \"all rights reserved.\" Students find this one funny once they notice nobody asked for legal text.\n* Invented specific details. Product names, prices, review quotes, user names. The AI doesn't leave blanks — it fills them with confident specifics.\n* AI doesn't adjust prices, so all will likely be in modern pricing equivalents\n* A section for featured or popular items\n* A big banner area at the top.\n* A button telling you to do something. \"Get Started,\" \"Order Now,\" \"Sign Up.\"\n* An explanation of how the service works. The AI assumes the concept needs explaining, even when it's obvious.\n* A joke about the time period. Even if nobody asks for humor, it appears anyway. In the food delivery scenario, for example, every single test made a joke about slow internet.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ee80079-2f7f-405f-b0af-d37b204b1265",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9e8a7fd-5ef4-466f-b303-5559e2383408",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This level can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of several of the lesson objectives: analyze patterns in AI-generated responses to explain how input prompts influence outputs; and, refine AI-generated outputs by iterating on prompts and recognizing patterns in responses"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9e8a7fd-5ef4-466f-b303-5559e2383408",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fab838dd-63ed-4623-8e5b-72ad5b9e7959",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** \nWhy are there so many repeated patterns across different websites that were created?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** \nStudents should account for two facts at once: every page in the room is different, and nearly every page contains the same handful of things nobody asked for. Students should recognize that the AI learned from an enormous number of real websites, and that wherever students did not specify something, it supplied whatever was most common in those examples. The repeated patterns sit exactly where students left gaps — nobody said what to call their company, so everybody got a company name.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fab838dd-63ed-4623-8e5b-72ad5b9e7959",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "075cacdf-5370-46c8-8f2f-e15de00efa93",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nAsk a few volunteers to complete this sentence. Then click to reveal how AI would most likely fill in the blank.\n\n**Say**: AI uses the computational skill of pattern recognition to generate its most likely response. For example, it reads a large amount of sentences that contain the phrase above and starts to recognize a pattern. \n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "075cacdf-5370-46c8-8f2f-e15de00efa93",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f9f68eb-d3d4-4901-92bb-bd0ec667ec42",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nDefine probability\n\n**Say**: AI models learn patterns from very large collections of data. When you give an AI an input, it uses those patterns to produce an output that is likely to fit. When you asked it to make a web page, it produced something that resembles the many pages it has seen. More than one output was possible. Probability describes how likely a particular output is. This is why your page and your neighbor's page are both different from each other and full of similar things.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f9f68eb-d3d4-4901-92bb-bd0ec667ec42",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "427cf386-6ed0-4e00-97a8-141dfd41abef",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nDefine abstraction\n\n**Say**: You decided which details to include in your prompts and which ones to leave out. This was an example of the computational skill of abstraction and it is one of the most important concepts in computer science. We all witnessed, however, that when working with AI, whenever you didn't supply details, AI took your abstraction and filled in details itself. \n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "427cf386-6ed0-4e00-97a8-141dfd41abef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6d6cfe4-68c2-4440-af37-c94be1de1eaa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "032cae99-78da-4ff8-af94-900b4c7d5b06",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Say**: Look at your page one more time. You picked your site. You picked three details. You decided what to change. That is abstraction — deciding which details matter. Everything else on that screen, the AI chose. And it chose whatever was most likely, which is why so many of your pages ended up with the same things on them. That is probability. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** \nWho decides: you or the AI?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** \nStudents should recognize that both they and the AI made decisions, and should be able to say where the line between them falls. They decided the scenario, the three details they carried forward, and what to change. The AI decided everything they left unspecified — the company name, the layout, the writing, the prices, the jokes. Students should be able to point at a specific example of each on their own page. Expect disagreement and let it stand. Students who answer \"the AI\" are noticing how much arrived unrequested. Students who answer \"you\" are noticing that nothing existed until they typed. Each is right about half of it, and hearing the two answers next to each other is what locates the boundary. \nDo not settle it into a tidy \"both.\" If a student says \"both,\" press once: \"Where's the line?\" A student has fully arrived when they can say something close to: the AI decided everything I didn't.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "032cae99-78da-4ff8-af94-900b4c7d5b06",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8ab23ac-33fb-4912-885d-c0c77f161b3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "838b90e0-5d4c-4d99-a1fa-da72d8cb81b9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nReview Objectives\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nReview the Question of the Day\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nReview the lesson vocabulary"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "838b90e0-5d4c-4d99-a1fa-da72d8cb81b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8ab23ac-33fb-4912-885d-c0c77f161b3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e3178dc-26e9-434e-aaed-58328831f855",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "How you handle vocabulary is up to you. These terms were introduced during the lesson at the moment students needed them, so this slide is a reference rather than a required beat. Choose whichever option fits your class and your clock:\n* Vocab Guide. A guide containing all vocabulary for the unit is available to print or distribute digitally. Students can add to it as terms come up across lessons.\n* Word wall. Have students add these terms to a shared classroom artifact. Over the unit it becomes a visible record of what the class has built, and students can refer back to it during later lessons.\n* Skip it. If pacing is tight or your students already have what they need, move on. \n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e3178dc-26e9-434e-aaed-58328831f855",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8ab23ac-33fb-4912-885d-c0c77f161b3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c283fac6-347b-4ab3-8fcf-307880153c72",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**To help students notice how patterns emerge from large sets of data and how those patterns make prediction possible — the foundation for how AI models learn from training data.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c283fac6-347b-4ab3-8fcf-307880153c72",
        "lesson_activity.key": "10aa64ae-9998-49a4-8d2d-4455c43334eb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ee212f4-1fa6-4419-903c-f66b83c19187",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine being in the world’s biggest group chat. If you had access to every message ever sent, what might you notice? Think about common phrases, emojis, or inside jokes you might notice.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ee212f4-1fa6-4419-903c-f66b83c19187",
        "lesson_activity.key": "10aa64ae-9998-49a4-8d2d-4455c43334eb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57028683-01b0-45bc-bf42-2dc5e8883488",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n* *What would happen if you read every message ever sent in a giant group chat?*\n* *Could you start predicting what people might say next? How?*\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that they would start noticing patterns — commonly used emojis, inside jokes, tone, and conversational flow — and that those patterns would let them predict what comes next. Reinforce that:\n* AI models learn the same way — by processing millions of examples.\n* AI doesn't \"think.\" It predicts based on patterns in what it has already seen.\n\n\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57028683-01b0-45bc-bf42-2dc5e8883488",
        "lesson_activity.key": "10aa64ae-9998-49a4-8d2d-4455c43334eb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dccd6133-c6f7-46e8-8134-54e0b60a1e68",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dccd6133-c6f7-46e8-8134-54e0b60a1e68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "10aa64ae-9998-49a4-8d2d-4455c43334eb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7edcc412-b134-489b-97b4-d5dcc7308fac",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** To demonstrate how neural networks predict text based on limited context and show how increasing context improves the accuracy of predictions. This activity also sets up the question the next activity answers: where do the AI's patterns come from?\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Place students in groups of three to five.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group one copy of the Unplugged Neural Networks activity guide, placed face down. Let students know to keep it face down until instructed to flip it over.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Introduce the activity. Make sure students understand that the purpose is to simulate how an AI model makes predictions with limited and full context.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Unplugged Neural Network"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7edcc412-b134-489b-97b4-d5dcc7308fac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3b30810-cbbd-4cb3-acb5-35718afb7a0a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this activity, your group simulates how an AI model predicts words. In Round 1, you only get to see the last three words of a sentence before predicting the next word. That's how older AI models worked — with very limited memory. In Round 2, you get to see the entire previous sentence before making your prediction — like modern AI models, which work with much more context. Let's see how that changes your predictions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3b30810-cbbd-4cb3-acb5-35718afb7a0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3e1798d5-37c2-4c93-9b1b-00f8defb2f30",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Direct students to complete Round 1 in their activity guide. Use the steps on the slide to explain the activity procedure and answer any questions. When everyone has added a word, instruct students to unfold the paper and read the full sentence aloud as a group. Have groups to compare their sentences to see how similar or different they were. For example, students' sentences could end up like this:\n- Space travel is \n- exciting \n- and \n- dangerous\n- animals \n- live.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Circulate to ensure students aren't peeking back at earlier words or the full sentence.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Round 2 in their activity guide. Use the steps on the slide to explain the activity procedure and answer any questions. When everyone has contributed, have each group read their full sentence aloud. Direct groups to compare their sentences to Round 1—how did having more context change the predictions? \n\nExample:\n- The exhausted student left school to go home to . . .*rest before starting homework*.\n- The exhausted student left school to go home to . . .*eat dinner with the family*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students read full sentences, listen for students noticing how more words made it easier to choose a logical next word. If students aren’t making the connection, ask: \"What helped you decide which word to add this time?\" and \"Was it easier or harder than the first round?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Due to the variety of answers possible, there is no exemplar provided for this activity. Reference the examples above for possible student generated sentences."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3e1798d5-37c2-4c93-9b1b-00f8defb2f30",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ac6518b9-69fc-424a-96f3-b4a06c031e71",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How did having limited context affect predictions?*\n- *What changed when you had access to the entire starting sentence and all words that were added before you?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:**Students recognize that limited context leads to less accurate and more random predictions, while access to more context helps predictions become more logical and connected to previous content. This mirrors how modern AI models improve their output by analyzing longer sequences of data.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ac6518b9-69fc-424a-96f3-b4a06c031e71",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44eaf5c5-b68e-41d5-91b2-5e2e9a387699",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students look at this slide. Explain that generative AI uses probabilities to predict what word should come next in a sentence. In this example, AI is most likely to predict the word \"presents\" or \"eyes\" next. AI is extremely unlikely to choose the word \"clouds,\" which is why the percentage is so small. Since this model has limited context — it only sees the words \"opened their\" — sometimes its predictions don't make sense in the context of what came before it. Connect this to Round 1, where students could only see the last three words before adding their word.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Make sure students understand that the percentages represent how likely AI is to choose that word to come next. In this example, AI is most likely to choose the word “presents” (40%) next. It is slightly less likely to choose “eyes” (30%) and much less likely to choose “mail” (18%), “door” (11%), or any other word."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44eaf5c5-b68e-41d5-91b2-5e2e9a387699",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93be5c10-736e-41a3-bb42-46b437ce0bf0",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students look at the Improved AI Predictions slide and note the differences between this slide and the previous slide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Why did AI’s predictions change? How does this relate to the neuron activity? \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to explain that increased context allows AI to make more appropriate predictions. Here, the inclusion of the words \"The students\" led the AI to make next-word predictions related to a school setting — just like Round 2, where seeing the full sentence made students' own predictions more logical.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *neural network*. Connect the term to the activity: each student acted as a neuron, and passing the paper mirrored how information flows through the network.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Vocabulary in this unit can be supported in whichever way works best for your classroom: have students record the definition in their Vocab Guide, add the term to your class word wall, or simply hold a quick discussion of the term as it comes up on the slide. Use the same routine you chose in Lesson 1 so students know what to expect.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93be5c10-736e-41a3-bb42-46b437ce0bf0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a359156-aed4-40b0-8cb4-f413ac99373b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** To give students hands-on experience training an AI model with labeled data. Students label images to teach an AI to recognize fish, then run the AI and see how the quality of their training determines what it can and can't do.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Train an AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a359156-aed4-40b0-8cb4-f413ac99373b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f9cdf25-c130-4bb7-8e1b-a8cc6e41763a",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just acted as a trained neural network. But where did your \"training\" come from? You've read thousands of sentences. How did the AI learn its patterns? Let's find out — by training an AI ourselves.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f9cdf25-c130-4bb7-8e1b-a8cc6e41763a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28f1371b-c5e9-4900-a678-7bcb1ca4507a",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pair students up. Each student trains their own AI. Before each Run, partners predict what their AIs do, then compare their results.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28f1371b-c5e9-4900-a678-7bcb1ca4507a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "847bfea2-7a4e-401a-a7a3-48df0c056177",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3 of the Train an AI activity.\n* **Level 1:** Students train the AI to identify fish by labeling images as \"fish\" or \"not fish,\" then run the AI to see how their training performs.\n* **Level 2:** Students see how their trained AI responds to sea creatures that belong in the water but aren't fish.\n* **Level 3:** Students train the AI to identify which objects belong in the water, then run it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for partners predicting before each Run and comparing results after — the prediction and comparison are where the thinking happens. If pairs are racing through labels, ask: \"What do you predict your AI does with this one? Why?\" When the two AIs disagree or one makes a mistake, ask: \"Why might your AIs have learned differently? Whose fault is a wrong answer — the AI's or the trainer's?\"\n",
        "progression_name": "Train an AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students finish early, encourage them to select Train More and add more training data, then run the AI again to see whether more examples change its accuracy.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "847bfea2-7a4e-401a-a7a3-48df0c056177",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fa95e81-eacf-40b6-b64b-4d7181ac9929",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *What did your AI learn? What did it get wrong?*\n* *Whose fault was that?*\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect the AI's behavior directly to the training they provided. The AI only learns what it is taught — if it flags a whale, that's because whales were never in its training examples, not because the AI \"glitched.\" Listen for students shifting blame from the AI (\"it made a mistake\") to the training (\"we never showed it one of those\"). This discussion sets up the definition of training on the next slide.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Different students' AIs make different mistakes depending on how they trained. Treat those differences as evidence, not errors — ask pairs whose AIs disagreed: \"Why might your two AIs have learned differently?\" This plants the idea the class digs into during the Train a New Word activity.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fa95e81-eacf-40b6-b64b-4d7181ac9929",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb99f7bc-ba1e-4e38-806e-182ed40fce8e",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *training*. Connect the term to what students just did: labeling images to teach the AI was training — the same process real AI models go through, except with millions of examples instead of a few dozen."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb99f7bc-ba1e-4e38-806e-182ed40fce8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55bc5420-7882-4131-b751-7ea46bff4657",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Training Data and Bias\" video.\n\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:**\n- How can the training data an AI learns from cause it to make mistakes — or be unfair?\n\n**Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that an AI's predictions are only as good as its training data. Incomplete or unrepresentative training data leads to mistakes and unfair outcomes — the AI has no way to know about anything it was never shown. Encourage students to connect this to their own AIs from the last activity: the mistakes their AIs made trace back to the training data they provided. This sets up the next activity, where students train the AI on a word that has no single right answer.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *training data*. Connect the term to the activity: the images students labeled as \"fish\" or \"not fish\" were the training data for their AI. Point out that the definition mentions text, images, and code — students used images, but an AI can be trained on any of these.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55bc5420-7882-4131-b751-7ea46bff4657",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbe99160-7709-4d48-8442-8ceeb9a130f9",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbe99160-7709-4d48-8442-8ceeb9a130f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b81e6d1-8b4b-4725-a372-0abaa83da637",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have partners agree on the same new word before they begin so they can compare results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 4 to complete Levels 4 through 6.\n* **Level 4:** Students choose a new word describing a color or shape and train the AI to recognize it.\n* **Level 5:** Students choose a word that describes an opinion, like \"fierce,\" and train the AI to recognize it.\n* **Level 6:** Students respond to the Check Your Understanding question.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** On Level 4, listen for students noticing that the AI learns a color or shape reliably. On Level 5, listen for hesitation — students often pause over whether a fish is \"fierce.\" Name that hesitation as important: \"You had to decide. What did you base it on?\" If partners' results differ, ask: \"You picked the same word. Why did your AIs learn it differently?\"",
        "progression_name": "Train a New Word",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Level 5 is the heart of this activity. Words like \"fierce\" have no single right answer, so each student's training reflects their own judgment. Expect partners' AIs to disagree — that disagreement is the point, not a mistake, and it leads directly into the next discussion.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 6 can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of the lesson objectives. Students explain why an AI trained to find trash flagged a whale, using the terms training and training data. Here are things to look for and actions you can take:\n* If students say the AI \"made a mistake\" or \"glitched\" without referencing its training, ask: \"Could the AI have recognized the whale? What would it have needed?\" Revisit the training data definition and the whale moment from the levels.\n* If students use the vocabulary but describe training as the AI \"looking things up\" or \"searching the internet,\" clarify that the AI only learns from the examples it was given during training — connect back to how their own AI only knew the fish they labeled.\n* If students correctly explain that the whale was missing from the training data, move on. For an extension, ask: \"What would you add to the training data to fix this — and could that create new problems?\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b81e6d1-8b4b-4725-a372-0abaa83da637",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fdeeb103-0ec4-4966-a8d6-9b536d16009c",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** You and your partner trained your AIs on the same word. Did your AIs learn it the same way? Why or why not?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that their AIs learned differently because they made different decisions while labeling. When the word described a color or shape, partners' AIs likely agreed. When the word described an opinion, like \"fierce,\" partners had to decide for themselves which fish counted — and their AIs learned whatever each of them decided. Listen for students articulating that the training data carries the opinions of the person who made it. Push further with: \"If you and your partner disagree about one word in one classroom, what happens when an AI is trained by people whose opinions differ from the people using it?\"\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fdeeb103-0ec4-4966-a8d6-9b536d16009c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b34249bf-f5da-422d-9fc7-b6ab6b0504aa",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Chatbots and Large Language Models\" video.\n\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:**\n- How is a large language model different from the AI you just trained?\n- You trained your AI on a few dozen fish. What is a chatbot trained on — and who decides what it learns?\n\n\n**Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that the AI they trained was built for one specific task — telling fish from trash — while a large language model is trained on an enormous body of text gathered from across the internet and can generate all kinds of new writing. Both learn the same way, from training data. The difference is scale and range. Students should also recognize that people shape what a chatbot learns: the text it trains on is collected by people, and people tune the model afterward to keep it from producing biased or harmful content. Listen for students connecting this back to their own experience — they decided what counted as \"fierce,\" and that decision shaped what their AI learned. The same kinds of human decisions are being made about chatbots, at a much larger scale.\n\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "LLM Synthesis"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b34249bf-f5da-422d-9fc7-b6ab6b0504aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e44638d-861a-4b06-8b90-a0b1d702bdce",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *large language model*. Connect the term to the video and to students' own experience: the AI Chat they used in Lesson 1 is a large language model. The AI they trained today learned from a few dozen images they labeled; an LLM learns from an enormous amount of text."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e44638d-861a-4b06-8b90-a0b1d702bdce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dcbcdd1d-1f55-4fb1-8639-d93ac9326a94"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d808a81-de39-4a51-998c-4546ee74158a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today. Let students know that they will be answering the question a little differently today.\n\n**Distribute:** Give each student an index card or a half sheet of scrap paper.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Direct students to create a three-panel comic strip that answers the Question of the Day. Let students know this is a creative way to synthesize the lesson — comics can be funny, serious, or weird, as long as they show something true about how AI learns.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students incorporating ideas from today — training data, what an AI can and can't recognize, or where its mistakes come from. If students are stuck, suggest they draw one moment from the lesson: an AI mislabeling a whale, two partners' AIs disagreeing about which fish is \"fierce,\" or their group's folded-paper sentence.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Three panels keeps this to about three minutes. Collect the cards on the way out as an exit ticket, or post a few on the class word wall alongside the vocabulary from this lesson.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d808a81-de39-4a51-998c-4546ee74158a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a891591d-2b5f-48a5-a6ea-4a1df6c7d190"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de1c8417-ecf6-439c-a6c3-f191b591e3b9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de1c8417-ecf6-439c-a6c3-f191b591e3b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a891591d-2b5f-48a5-a6ea-4a1df6c7d190"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "11508ca8-0e3c-4226-b7a2-0534a5a9d7a5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students encounter AI-generated misinformation before it has a name. They leave the warm up unable to tell which statement is false, which creates the need for retrieval information in the Activity.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the three statements. Direct students to discuss with a partner which statements they think are true and which are not. After a minute or two, facilitate a quick vote by having students hold up fingers for the statement they think is false. Ask two or three students to explain their reasoning.\n",
        "name": "Truth or Lies? ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Do not reveal the answers. Students verify the statements themselves in the activity. Tell them they find out who is right once they get on their computers. Avoid naming or defining hallucination here — students name the concept after they experience it.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "11508ca8-0e3c-4226-b7a2-0534a5a9d7a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "609a90f9-32a7-452b-8465-d2e27e5eb5fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75131662-5e21-4511-b037-061d52ff8d02",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75131662-5e21-4511-b037-061d52ff8d02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "609a90f9-32a7-452b-8465-d2e27e5eb5fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efe017b5-5811-4d71-a532-25149773943f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students experience an AI chatbot stating false information confidently, then see how attaching a document changes its answers. This firsthand experience becomes the basis for defining hallucination and retrieval information.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 1. Students ask the chatbot for information about octopuses and try to determine whether what it tells them is true. They then attach the Octopus Fact Sheet and ask again, using the document to check the statements from the warm up. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** While students work without the document, listen for frustration. The bot answers confidently and does not admit uncertainty, which is intentional. Ask students how they could check whether one of its facts is true. Once students attach the fact sheet, look for students who notice the bot correcting information it gave earlier in the same conversation. Point this out to students who miss it.\n\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Fact-Check the Bot",
        "progression_name": "Fact-Check the Bot",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Give students time to work without the document before they attach it. The time spent trying to get a straight answer is what makes the change obvious once the fact sheet is added. Students who attach the file immediately miss the contrast.\n\nIf time allows, poll the class to see who guessed correctly during the warm up. The false statement was the one about the giant Pacific octopus living more than 30 years — the fact sheet states that it lives three to five years.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efe017b5-5811-4d71-a532-25149773943f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3e006554-00cd-4caf-891c-5f6d9c15b377",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3e006554-00cd-4caf-891c-5f6d9c15b377",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c359ad7e-1a6e-4681-806c-8a8b1759772e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** \nClick through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *Same bot, same questions — what changed?*\n* *Why couldn't it give you straight answers before adding the file?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that the chatbot did not change and did not become smarter — what changed is that it had a reliable source of information to draw from. Listen for students describing the bot as guessing, making things up, or saying whatever sounded right before the document was added. These descriptions become the definitions on the next two slides.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students have not been given the words **hallucination** or **retrieval information** yet. Let them describe what happened in their own language. If a student uses one of the terms already, acknowledge it and tell the class you define both words in a moment.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c359ad7e-1a6e-4681-806c-8a8b1759772e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db7cd58f-6448-4fda-823a-e4d4b4b5f600",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db7cd58f-6448-4fda-823a-e4d4b4b5f600",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e376bfdf-bb8f-4fa2-bfd5-c8a4a03e810d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *hallucination*. Connect the definition to what students just experienced. Remind them that the chatbot stated that the giant Pacific octopus lives more than 30 years with complete confidence, and gave students no signal that the fact was wrong. That confidence is what makes hallucinations hard to catch.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain why the hallucinations students encountered today happened."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e376bfdf-bb8f-4fa2-bfd5-c8a4a03e810d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9670e2e9-19a8-4507-8a32-79a7f9fccd11",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI doesn't \"think\" like a human — it predicts based on probability and patterns in its training data. When it told you something false about octopuses, it wasn't lying on purpose; it generated a statement that sounded right, because sounding right is what it's built to do. That's why it was so hard to spot. When you attached the document, you gave it real information to draw from instead of guessing.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students ask why the AI got better once the file was attached, connect back to their experience: the AI didn't get smarter — it stopped predicting from patterns alone and started referencing the document. That distinction sets up the next activity, where students control what the bot references.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9670e2e9-19a8-4507-8a32-79a7f9fccd11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5a2fa34-4d53-4248-8ece-ae72471f4cd7",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Define retrieval information. Connect this back to when students attached the Octopus Fact Sheet, which gave the chatbot additional information. The bot stopped guessing about octopuses and started using the document instead. That's why it's answers changed.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students already used the word retrieval in the level instructions before they had a definition for it. Point that out — they gave the bot retrieval information before they had a name for it. \n\nIn the next activity, students go from attaching retrieval information to deciding what retrieval information a bot gets. That shift is the heart of the rest of the lesson.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5a2fa34-4d53-4248-8ece-ae72471f4cd7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "690b57c7-b22f-43f6-9681-bff40fb95cbd",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \n Explain that AI sometimes gives conflicting answers to the same question. This happens for two reasons. Sometimes AI makes something up. Other times the information AI was given disagrees with itself — one source says one thing and another source says something different. When that happens, a well-built chatbot tells you the sources disagree instead of picking one.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students encounter this in the next activity, where their chatbot reports conflicting information. Keep this brief — contradiction is not a key vocabulary term in this lesson, so students do not need to record a definition.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "690b57c7-b22f-43f6-9681-bff40fb95cbd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "79305b24-7a88-4405-ab1d-9ee6b803d441",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \n Click through the animated slide to explain that adding retrieval information improves AI responses but does not guarantee they are correct. AI can only use the information it's given, so if that information is wrong or out of date, the response is too — and AI gives no signal that anything is wrong.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This is the idea students test in the next activity. Their bot has retrieval information, but some of it is bad, and the bot cannot tell the difference. Students have to make that judgment themselves.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "79305b24-7a88-4405-ab1d-9ee6b803d441",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dab2da31-7ae3-4f73-8b1d-83fe239e51cb",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** \n Students decide what information a chatbot gets, discovering that a bot with bad retrieval information is unreliable even though it has additional information. This shifts them from using retrieval information to judging it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**\n Direct students to Lesson 3, Levels 2-3. \n* **Level 2:** Students choose one of four bots to fix, ask it questions, and after finding that its answers are unreliable, remove or edit the retrieval information to improve their bot.\n* **Level 3:** Students answer check for understanding questions. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** \n Look for students who never open the Retrieval panel. Chat alone reveals only the conflicting information — the vague, off-topic, and undated cards are found by reading. Also watch for students who delete every card that looks suspicious. One card is half accurate and half outdated, and deleting it loses information the bot needs. Ask those students whether they lost anything they needed. Two cards are genuine judgment calls: one is accurate but not related to this bot's purpose, and one has no date, so there's no way to know whether it's current. Ask students to explain their reasoning rather than looking for a right answer.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Make a Trustworthy Bot",
        "progression_name": "Make a Trustworthy Bot",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Everything students need is already in the Retrieval panel. If you see students searching online during this part, redirect them — the optional challenge at the end of the level is where searching belongs.\n\nStudents who pick the dog care bot or the kitchen bot encounter retrieval cards with unsafe advice, such as chocolate being safe for dogs or chicken being safe when only the outside is browned. State the correct information out loud during the discussion so students leave with accurate understanding.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "These levels can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of the lesson objectives. \n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dab2da31-7ae3-4f73-8b1d-83fe239e51cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67c88d63-2020-4fa5-96da-bb681c9ff49f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** \nClick through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *Your bot only knew what someone gave it. Who decides what the AI tools you use already know?*\n* *How would you know if an AI you use had bad information?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** \nStudents recognize that every AI tool they use was given information by someone, and that person made the same kinds of choices students just made. Listen for students naming who those people might be — companies, developers, whoever writes or selects the source material. On the second question, listen for strategies rather than certainty: checking a fact against a reliable source, noticing when an answer sounds out of date, or asking where the information came from.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may conclude they can never trust AI. Redirect toward what they practiced today: they judged which information was worth keeping, and that's the same judgment they can apply to any AI tool. The goal is careful use, not avoidance.\n\nIf time is short, this discussion can fold into the Wrap Up alongside the Question of the Day.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67c88d63-2020-4fa5-96da-bb681c9ff49f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8583a78d-f7e9-4d69-80d4-3074e0db94d9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44493307-958b-474e-b7bf-40554e1446cd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to answer both halves using a specific example from the lesson — the octopus fact they couldn't verify, the moment the bot corrected itself after they attached the fact sheet, or a retrieval card they removed from their bot. Then invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students naming prediction as the reason AI makes things up, and retrieval information as the way to reduce it — while recognizing that retrieval information doesn't guarantee a correct answer.\n\n",
        "name": "Question of the Day Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, skip the partner talk and collect two or three quick examples from the whole class instead — one about why AI made things up, one about how retrieval information helped, and one about why they'd still check. This makes sure all three threads get named before students leave.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44493307-958b-474e-b7bf-40554e1446cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c17f29e-5de8-4d77-9c2d-2e126928473c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ba010ba1-6feb-4eba-a020-e089b66a6438",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ba010ba1-6feb-4eba-a020-e089b66a6438",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c17f29e-5de8-4d77-9c2d-2e126928473c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af3d7bb2-b9ce-43bd-a6c8-61d20559aea7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af3d7bb2-b9ce-43bd-a6c8-61d20559aea7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c17f29e-5de8-4d77-9c2d-2e126928473c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47ba9b56-cb4e-4bdc-9689-c6f0e09fb991",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** To spark students’ thinking about how AI-generated content can raise questions of fairness, originality, and trust — especially when it blurs the line between human and machine-made work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the warm-up by explaining that students will see real examples of how AI is used to create things like songs, videos, or writing. For each example, give students approximately 30 seconds to decide whether it feels real and fair or misleading and unfair. Then, have them show a thumbs up if it feels real and fair, a thumbs down if it feels misleading and unfair, or a sideways thumb if they’re unsure or in the middle. Let students know you’ll call on a few volunteers after each round to briefly share why they chose their position.",
        "name": "The Reality Check: Real and Fair or Misleading and Unfair?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This warm-up is meant to spark curiosity and preview the ethical challenges they’ll explore more deeply in the main activity — not to settle any debates yet. Keep the pace brisk: just 30 seconds for students to decide, then a quick reason from 1–2 volunteers. Don’t let discussion go too deep here — remind students they’ll have more time to dig into these ideas during the station activity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47ba9b56-cb4e-4bdc-9689-c6f0e09fb991",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d98f89c5-378a-4c47-8484-591dd579fafa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2bee4bb-2008-40fc-a3a2-e497847cfdb7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the scenario aloud: “AI-generated news article.” Give students about 30 seconds to decide whether this example feels real and fair or misleading and unfair. Then, them show a thumb up, down or sideways. Call on a few students to briefly explain their reasoning. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Thumbs up if you think an AI-generated news article feels real and fair. Thumbs down if you think it feels misleading and unfair. Thumbs in the middle if you are unsure.*\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students begin to question the trustworthiness and authorship of AI-generated content. This example invites discussion about whether AI-generated writing should be trusted, who’s accountable for errors, and whether it’s ethical to present AI-written news without clear labeling.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You might mention the real-world case of CNET, which published dozens of AI-written articles in 2023. Many contained factual errors or plagiarism and led to public backlash. Ask students, “If the article looks professional but it is wrong, who should be held responsible?”"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2bee4bb-2008-40fc-a3a2-e497847cfdb7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d98f89c5-378a-4c47-8484-591dd579fafa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e61add8-82b8-4ea4-be77-72e4f69a498b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Read the scenario aloud: “AI-generated audio recording based on someone’s actual voice.” Give students about 30 seconds to decide whether this example feels real and fair or misleading and unfair. Then, them show a thumb up, down or sideways. Call on a few students to briefly explain their reasoning. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Thumbs up if you think an AI-generated audio recording based on someone’s actual voice feels real and fair. Thumbs down if you think it feels misleading and unfair. Thumbs in the middle if you are unsure.*\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students recognize that AI-generated voice recordings raise serious questions about consent, impersonation, and control over identity. They should begin to consider whether it’s ethical to mimic someone’s voice — especially when used in entertainment or media — without permission.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You can reference the real 2023 example of an AI-generated song using the voices of Drake and The Weeknd that went viral — even though neither artist participated. Ask students, “If an AI uses someone’s voice without their permission, who benefits — and who’s harmed?”\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e61add8-82b8-4ea4-be77-72e4f69a498b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d98f89c5-378a-4c47-8484-591dd579fafa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a310cefb-add5-4300-b311-66c60d188945",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Read the scenario aloud: “AI filter or airbrushed photo.” Give students about 30 seconds to decide whether this example feels real and fair or misleading and unfair. Then, them show a thumb up, down or sideways. Call on a few students to briefly explain their reasoning. \n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Thumbs up if you think an AI filter or airbrushed photo feels real and fair. Thumbs down if you think it feels misleading and unfair. Thumbs in the middle if you are unsure.*\n\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Encourage students to think critically about how AI tools for filtering or retouching photos influence our perceptions of reality, beauty, and authenticity. They might surface ideas about consent (when someone's image is altered), self-representation, or pressure to meet unrealistic standards. You can also connect this to broader questions of what counts as original or truthful in creative work.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a310cefb-add5-4300-b311-66c60d188945",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d98f89c5-378a-4c47-8484-591dd579fafa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6caa79bc-9d8c-4d45-827d-5d1a10796e48",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *If AI content (like a deepfake video or audio) looks or sounds like a real person, but a real person didn’t create it, who should get credit or control?*\n- *What makes an AI creation feel unfair to the original person or artist involved?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students start thinking critically about representation and ownership. By referencing deepfakes explicitly, the class can discuss what happens when AI convincingly imitates someone’s voice or face — and how that differs from AI remixing someone’s creative work. This sets up both the vocabulary (like attribution and copyright) and the upcoming station questions about credit, control, and fairness. \n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6caa79bc-9d8c-4d45-827d-5d1a10796e48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d98f89c5-378a-4c47-8484-591dd579fafa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "406d4dfd-43a5-4d4b-b9ab-39c26590901d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If we can’t tell the difference between what AI makes and what people make, how should we handle credit, permission, and payment? That’s exactly what we’re going to explore in the next activity.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "406d4dfd-43a5-4d4b-b9ab-39c26590901d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d98f89c5-378a-4c47-8484-591dd579fafa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e64c193f-da83-4bcb-8507-42bd30d526f4",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e64c193f-da83-4bcb-8507-42bd30d526f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d98f89c5-378a-4c47-8484-591dd579fafa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99c4a596-b036-440e-9b3f-bb0b5ab30ebe",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students analyze real-world AI controversies to explore the ethical tradeoffs between innovation and creative ownership. They consider issues like consent, credit, and compensation while hearing multiple perspectives and forming their own opinions, then put names to the ideas they argued about.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *copyright*. Let students know this topic comes up again at one of the stations.\n\n\n\n",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Ownership and AI Stations",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Let students know this is a legal concept, not just about giving credit — it’s about who has the right to decide how a piece of work is used. You might find it helpful to tell students that if they create something, they decide whether others can copy it or profit from it. That legal control is what copyright protects.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99c4a596-b036-440e-9b3f-bb0b5ab30ebe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ee788f63-a902-4258-8f3f-a1e1d26b438c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the EU AI Act as an example of how governments regulate AI. Point out that it requires companies to say if they used copyrighted work and lets creators ask to opt out. Use the pyramid graphic to explain how the Act assigns different rules based on an AI system's risk level, with higher-risk systems requiring stronger ethical protections.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask students whether an opt-out is enough. Should creators have to ask not to be included, or should companies have to ask permission first? Keep this to about thirty seconds — students weigh in properly at the stations. The opt-out versus opt-in question returns at Station C, where they consider whether musicians should control AI trained on their music.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ee788f63-a902-4258-8f3f-a1e1d26b438c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d00a4429-2501-482c-b9f0-2947f0740446",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Earlier you gave a thumbs up or down on AI news articles, voice clones, and filters. Now let's explore the bigger question: How can we balance the benefits of AI with fairness to original creators? At each station, you see a real controversy and weigh in with your own perspective.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d00a4429-2501-482c-b9f0-2947f0740446",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a4eb26a-1dfc-49d7-a25d-d3480840d31f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Place students in groups of 2-3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Ownership and AI Stations activity guide.\n\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Setup guidance — what to print, how to arrange the stations, and how many sticky notes each station needs — is in the Ownership and AI Stations teacher resource. Set the stations up before class.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a4eb26a-1dfc-49d7-a25d-d3480840d31f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09cdb359-52e9-495c-bcdf-0319ff08821f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the Ownership and AI Stations and the steps for this activity. Direct students to complete the stations with their groups. Students have about two to three minutes per station, and they do not need to visit the stations in order.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate**:  As students work, listen for nuanced reasoning and respectful disagreements. Encourage them to use terms like consent, credit, copyright, and attribution in their discussions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to go back to the station they started at for about 1 minute to take a look at the sticky placement and read their peer’s sticky note comments. Once time is up, direct students to the station they went to second and repeat. Remind students to write down any new ideas or reflections they have on their activity guide as they visit each station.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Timing:** Before students rotate, give them a 10-second countdown to finish writing and posting. After students complete all stations, allow them to revisit their first and second stations to see new responses. (They don’t need to revisit their third station — they just came from it!)\n\n**Use the gif:** While going through the instructions for the stations, it may be helpful to either talk through the gif on the slide or demonstrate the flow to ensure students understand that they will be writing on and putting two sticky notes on each poster.\n- The first sticky note at each station will be used to place on a scale or on a chart after they write their reasons for their placement on that first sticky note\n- The second sticky note at each station will be used to write their answer to the second question and they will place this second sticky note under the question on the poster.\n\nYou can determine whether you would like students to put their names on their sticky notes to track participation. If you decide to have students add their names, it is suggested that they put their names on the back of the sticky notes so that students are more focused on the responses, rather than who wrote what.\n\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09cdb359-52e9-495c-bcdf-0319ff08821f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5dd7f12-21b0-4e23-afd3-7abce88e26a2",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Direct students’ attention to Station A. Remind them of the scenario they read there: AI-generated books listed on Amazon under fake author names.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Have a quick discussion about the placement of sticky notes on the different quadrants. What do they notice? What do they wonder? Click through the animated slide to display the second question, and pick out a couple of sticky notes from the second question on the poster to help drive the discussion of ownership and copyright.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The primary goal of this discussion is for students to explore how AI-generated content raises questions about ownership and copyright. They should consider who should control or benefit from content created by AI — especially when it is trained on human-created works. Students may mention shared ownership, consent, and whether companies or users should have legal rights to something built on other people’s creative work. Students have the word copyright now, so listen for whether they use it precisely — as legal control over how a work is used, not just as credit.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To keep the station debriefs tight, try to spend about one to two minutes per station's poster and focus the discussions on the second question. Each poster is followed by a slide that picks up the thread, so there is no need to resolve the debate here.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5dd7f12-21b0-4e23-afd3-7abce88e26a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b527aa7d-8225-4e93-9a12-c36b700a6967",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Use the slide to explain that in 2023 artists and authors sued AI companies for using their copyrighted works without permission, one of the strongest reactions to AI training practices. Note that the outcome is still unfolding: one case ended in a settlement worth $1.5 billion, and most others remain undecided. Connect this back to Station A — this is what actually happened when AI companies used copyrighted material to train their models. Encourage students to consider that this is not only a legal question but one about livelihoods.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students often assume a lawsuit means someone broke the law. Courts have not settled whether training AI on copyrighted work is legal — that is exactly what these cases are deciding. If a student asks \"so is it illegal?\", the honest answer is that no one knows yet, and that uncertainty is part of why the ethical question matters."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b527aa7d-8225-4e93-9a12-c36b700a6967",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "250fcbcd-ed0f-459a-8f46-84e2ef08eded",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "250fcbcd-ed0f-459a-8f46-84e2ef08eded",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2ce26f11-b22f-4d31-834a-89fc649b6bc3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Direct students’ attention to Station B. Remind them of the scenario they read there: an AI-generated artwork made with MidJourney won first place at the Colorado State Fair's digital art competition in 2022.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Have a quick discussion about the placement of sticky notes along the real to not real scale. What do they notice? What do they wonder? Click through the animated slide to display the second question, and pick out a couple of sticky notes from the second question on the poster to help drive the discussion of authorship and creativity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The primary goal of this discussion is for students to reflect on how AI-generated art challenges definitions of authorship and creativity. They should consider attribution — who deserves credit — when a person provides a prompt, but the machine generates the final output. They may connect this to whether the AI itself, the programmer, or the user should be seen as the \"creator,\" and whether competitions should require disclosure or separate AI-assisted work from human work.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students do not have the word attribution yet — it comes on the next slide. Let them argue in their own words here. Naming the idea after they have wrestled with it is the point.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2ce26f11-b22f-4d31-834a-89fc649b6bc3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5299acd-4127-4238-8c1a-5bc9bc5b306b",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *attribution*. Name it as the word for what students were just arguing about at Station B — who deserves credit when a person writes the prompt and the machine makes the picture.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Attribution does not give a creator control; it recognizes their contribution, and is often seen as a basic form of respect in creative work. Pair this with copyright from earlier: copyright is legal control over how a work is used, attribution is credit. A creator can have one without the other, and that gap is exactly what students were arguing about at Station B.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5299acd-4127-4238-8c1a-5bc9bc5b306b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1a7e7481-9fd6-454a-a5e5-4746159acbde",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Direct students’ attention to Station C. Remind them of the scenario they read there: major music companies sued the AI music platforms Suno and Udio over songs used without permission.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Have a quick discussion about the placement of sticky notes on the Yes or No table. What do they notice? What do they wonder? Click through the animated slide to display the second question, and pick out a couple of sticky notes from the second question on the poster to help drive the discussion of compensation, consent, and whether musicians can even find out their music was used. Close the station by asking, \"Later in this unit, you use AI to help create something of your own. Does that change your answer about paying musicians?\"\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The primary goal of this discussion is for students to analyze how AI-generated music raises concerns about compensation and consent. They should explore whether musicians should have control over how their work is used to train AI, and who deserves to be paid when that training leads to profitable outputs. Students may debate whether using someone's style or voice without permission violates ethical or legal standards, even if the AI output is new. The closing question asks students to test their position against their own future choices. Expect some students to shift once they picture themselves as the one using the tool. Name that shift out loud rather than resolving it — the tension is the point.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The next two slides give students the word for the last thread here — that musicians cannot always find out whether their music was used — so there is no need to settle that part of the discussion now.\n\nIf students ask how the lawsuits turned out: two of the major music companies have since settled and partnered with the AI platforms to build licensed music tools, where artists choose whether their voice and songs can be used. Ask students whether that counts as a fair outcome for musicians.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1a7e7481-9fd6-454a-a5e5-4746159acbde",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b203ae4f-4e81-4948-b2cc-956d86155883",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *transparency*. Name it as the word for what students landed on at Station C — musicians cannot find out whether their music was used to train an AI. Let students know the next slide shows what one company's transparency actually looks like.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Transparency matters when people do not know whether their work is being used, or how an AI system makes decisions. This helps students understand why a lack of transparency can lead to ethical problems, especially when creators never learn they were involved.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b203ae4f-4e81-4948-b2cc-956d86155883",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63070b6b-551c-4ff7-acfa-2e5902a67ab1",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide. Students have just defined transparency — this slide asks them to judge a real example of it. Explain that creators' complaints about transparency led to real laws: California's AI training data transparency law took effect in January 2026, and the European Union has a similar requirement through the AI Act students saw earlier. Explain that OpenAI's summary describes four broad categories of data and states that it includes copyrighted material, but does not list the specific works. Ask students whether a summary is enough, and what real transparency looks like.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "OpenAI is one example here, not an outlier. Every major AI developer publishes a summary that describes categories rather than listing sources. Frame this as a pattern across the industry rather than one company behaving badly, so students focus on the policy question instead of on a single company.\n\nListen for students naming what is missing: a searchable list, a notification to creators, or a way to opt out. If students stall, ask why a company might not want to name specific sources, and whether any of those reasons are fair to creators. Leave the question open — the next slide names the bigger idea underneath it, and students write their own answer in the Check Your Understanding.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63070b6b-551c-4ff7-acfa-2e5902a67ab1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6ca31e1-4d9f-4528-8ff0-ac49dfa640f0",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *ethics*. Connect it back to the stations — every question students weighed in on today was an ethics question, even the ones that felt like legal questions. Let students know they use this idea in the next activity when they name a value of their own.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Ask students to point to a moment at one of the stations where something felt wrong to them even though it might be perfectly legal. That gap between what is legal and what is right is what ethics names. Copyright and the lawsuits covered the legal side of today; ethics is the word for everything students argued about that the law does not settle."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6ca31e1-4d9f-4528-8ff0-ac49dfa640f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2e87db23-6648-45ae-98b4-d6ef8c5d5d5f",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** To give students a chance to reflect on the ethical tradeoffs discussed at each station and articulate a value they think should guide how AI interacts with human-created content.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Navigate to Lesson 4, Level 1 on Code Studio in order to answer the free response reflection question. Remind students that they just named the gap between what is legal and what is right — this is where they name the value that matters most to them.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this self-assessment to gauge how well students understand the ethical tradeoffs involved in AI development. As students respond, look for whether they:\n- Identify a clear value (e.g., attribution, transparency, consent) that reflects an ethical concern\n- Reference or imply key terms like attribution, transparency, compensation, or ownership\n- Demonstrate awareness of both benefits and risks of using human-created work in AI systems\n- Show consideration for who benefits and who may be left out when AI is trained on others’ work\nIf many responses are vague or one-sided (e.g., “AI should always be allowed to use whatever data it wants”), consider doing one or more of the following:\n- Revisit one of the case studies from earlier in the lesson and discuss: “What might this creator say about that value?”\n- Have students revisit the vocabulary from today — in their Vocab Guide, on the word wall, or on the Key Vocabulary slides — and identify which terms apply to their response\nUse a class discussion to explore a range of student\n- Use a class discussion to explore a range of student responses and ask students to challenge or build on one another’s reasoning\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2e87db23-6648-45ae-98b4-d6ef8c5d5d5f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5303dbca-de47-4f28-a985-8a871eadb0a3",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5303dbca-de47-4f28-a985-8a871eadb0a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53771985-df37-46fa-a314-5cd211871188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74524881-4ef8-4dac-b218-3179a59914ba",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Return to one of the Reality Check scenarios from the Warm Up and have students vote again with a thumb up, down, or sideways. Ask whether anyone's thumb moved, and call on two or three students to name the ethical consideration that changed their thinking.\n\n\n\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Student Survey",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students wrote a value statement in Level 1 moments ago, so this wrap up is deliberately verbal — no one needs to write anything again. Revisiting the Warm Up vote makes students' own shifts visible and answers the Question of the Day with evidence rather than a restatement.\n\nIf the thumbs routine feels stale by this point, a quick whip around works just as well: each student names one ethical consideration in a word or short phrase, moving fast around the room. Either way, keep this to two or three minutes so the Key Vocabulary slides are not rushed.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74524881-4ef8-4dac-b218-3179a59914ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "80eda306-2ee3-454f-ab95-e68b68732692"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be94490c-211d-46e6-8ac8-de8aaf3805c0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be94490c-211d-46e6-8ac8-de8aaf3805c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "80eda306-2ee3-454f-ab95-e68b68732692"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e86bddea-6afa-4c52-a91c-01ae06460996",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI models learn by analyzing vast amounts of data. They recognize patterns and use them to make predictions. This is an example of an AI that is trained to play rock, paper, scissors. It has been trained on images of hands and has to predict what the user played.\n",
        "name": "AI Assumptions",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e86bddea-6afa-4c52-a91c-01ae06460996",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ccf1b25-8363-4f85-87cf-204c861265dc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "684fdf17-c747-4173-9644-9eb2331a16c4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students if AI would label this image as rock, paper, or scissors? Discuss how it looks similar to other hands in the “Paper” category so AI would likely label this as “Paper” as well. ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Ask students to identify patterns in the data. For example, the AI Bot might recognize this hand as “Paper” because it sees 5 outstretched fingers. It might recognize that “Scissors” has two outstretched fingers, and “Rock” has none. "
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "684fdf17-c747-4173-9644-9eb2331a16c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ccf1b25-8363-4f85-87cf-204c861265dc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6b28623-177b-4dd2-bd9c-9458438d4a3a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6b28623-177b-4dd2-bd9c-9458438d4a3a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ccf1b25-8363-4f85-87cf-204c861265dc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "525fe694-d17c-4f64-8c17-be1448576d9f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Ask students how AI might label this image.*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Explain that AI might incorrectly label this image because it was not trained on images of hands held up in the air and was only trained on images of hands straight out. This is an example of bias in the training data, because the AI Bot was only trained to recognize hands from certain angles.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "525fe694-d17c-4f64-8c17-be1448576d9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ccf1b25-8363-4f85-87cf-204c861265dc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71e7459d-ab11-4731-b229-c856b638fc25",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What, or who, is not represented by these images? Give examples of some images that should be added to this dataset to improve the AI model.*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that AI models learn patterns and replicate biases. In order to to reduce or eliminate bias, more images should be added to the training dataset. Examples of images that should be added include, hands from different angles, hands missing fingers, hands with rings or other jewelry, etc.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you have time, test out this trained model from Teachable Machine: https://teachablemachine.withgoogle.com/models/fadF6RxOcF/. \n\nThis model was built with Kaggle data from thousands of pictures of hands: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/drgfreeman/rockpaperscissors. Note, this dataset primarily features images of the back of hands held straight up in the air and shows bias against hands viewed from the side or from the palm.\n\nAs an extension activity, consider creating your own Rock, Paper, Scissors model using Teachable Machine: https://teachablemachine.withgoogle.com/\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71e7459d-ab11-4731-b229-c856b638fc25",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ccf1b25-8363-4f85-87cf-204c861265dc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e338031b-0c41-4585-aaa8-e39876edacd3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e338031b-0c41-4585-aaa8-e39876edacd3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ccf1b25-8363-4f85-87cf-204c861265dc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cce28430-f904-4ee2-b33a-adbade3b811b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore AI's assumptions and analyze how training data influences AI-generated responses. Students analyze AI's initial responses, identify potential biases, and refine their prompts to create more neutral and inclusive descriptions.\n\n\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Bias Intro: Career Clothing"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cce28430-f904-4ee2-b33a-adbade3b811b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d876e7b-6050-459f-8e70-e8350b5b1676",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI learns from patterns in data, but that data isn't always balanced or fair. If certain perspectives or groups are overrepresented, AI will reflect those patterns in its responses. This can lead to AI making assumptions that don’t accurately represent the full picture.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d876e7b-6050-459f-8e70-e8350b5b1676",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c0e421e0-4ccb-4004-91f8-6ad15706c676",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *AI bias*.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you choose to demonstrate doing a Google Image search for “nurse” or “doctor”, consider searching for animations, e.g. \"nurse cartoon image”, to see more obvious bias."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c0e421e0-4ccb-4004-91f8-6ad15706c676",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb184e28-93f1-433d-817c-1170c6aeae15",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Place students in pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair of students a copy of the Understanding Bias activity guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb184e28-93f1-433d-817c-1170c6aeae15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "981c2c23-b341-4c38-b9e5-083e67be827c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI generates responses based on patterns in its training data. But what happens when those patterns reflect assumptions about people and professions? Let's investigate by asking AI to describe outfits for two different careers and analyzing its responses.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "981c2c23-b341-4c38-b9e5-083e67be827c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9bad62a3-89af-4a4d-927e-aff762ed85dd",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1 to explore AI-generated descriptions of clothing worn by different professions – a secretary and a CEO. Students should complete the AI Bias Analysis section of their activity guide as they interact with each task.",
        "progression_name": "Career Clothing",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "It might be hard to see overt bias in AI Chat’s responses. Consider discussing any nuanced biases which exist in society that are being reflected by the chatbot. For example, the chatbot returns style suggestions for both male and female CEOs but the suggestions differ by gender. Female CEOs are encouraged to wear skirts, dresses, heels, and flats but not chinos, button down shirts, or loafers. This reflects bias in our societal norms that the chatbot is parroting back to us."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9bad62a3-89af-4a4d-927e-aff762ed85dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c114c8c-6a8c-45b2-9c74-f338e0081756",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Did AI assume a default identity in either of the prompts?*\n- *Did the responses differ in the tone, assumptions, or characteristics included?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that AI descriptions reflect patterns in training data, which may reinforce stereotypes or assumptions. They should identify differences in language, gender associations, or implied expertise in AI-generated responses.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to articulate differences, prompt them with:\n- \"What words or phrases stand out in each description?\"\n- \"Does AI use the same level of professionalism, detail, or authority in both responses?\"\n- \"Would changing the wording of the prompt lead to a different response?\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c114c8c-6a8c-45b2-9c74-f338e0081756",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6bcf33b6-10b6-4094-9bbd-1478fbdb81fb",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6bcf33b6-10b6-4094-9bbd-1478fbdb81fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25134bba-9058-4adc-80fc-e47b5c7c817c",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've seen how AI describes outfits for different professions, let's take it a step further. AI doesn't generate biased responses on purpose—it reflects patterns in the data it has learned from. But we can adjust our inputs to get better, more neutral results. Let's try to refine our prompts and see how AI responds.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25134bba-9058-4adc-80fc-e47b5c7c817c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09914e9f-dc5c-4e20-841d-1da02ed00301",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This**: Return students to Lesson 5, Level 1. Refine the original prompts to improve neutrality. Students document their refined prompts and compare AI's new responses in the AI Bias Analysis Chart section of their activity guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09914e9f-dc5c-4e20-841d-1da02ed00301",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53fd29d0-a46e-479a-bc69-fd5501256b1f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What factors in AI's training data may have led to this bias we uncovered?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students to bring up bias in the datasets, connecting back to the warm up activity, other examples they may be familiar with, or their own lived experiences.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53fd29d0-a46e-479a-bc69-fd5501256b1f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6353d0d1-68ad-4291-b7e7-5b97292abecb",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We've explored how AI bias emerges and how we can adjust prompts to reduce it. Now, let's think about what AI developers can do to make AI systems more fair and representative.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6353d0d1-68ad-4291-b7e7-5b97292abecb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a751befa-3a76-4b7e-9ed9-8e68eca7c3ab",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition**: Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 2 to complete the formative assessment.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \n- Students answer the multiple-choice question: \"How could AI developers make adjustments to reduce bias?\"\n- Use the \"View Student Responses\" feature to analyze the class results.\n- Ask students: \"What do you notice?\" and \"What do you wonder?\" Have them discuss with a partner before selecting a few volunteers to share.\n",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that AI bias is not just a coding issue—it's a data issue. Many students assume developers can \"fix\" bias with a simple programming change, but bias originates from the datasets AI is trained on."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This activity helps assess student understanding of AI bias mitigation strategies. Based on student responses, take the following actions:\n- If most students select the correct answer (B), proceed to the next activity (AI Audit).\n- If some students are confused, have them pair up and explain their reasoning to each other. Then, re-pose the question and have students show their answers using their fingers (1-4) before moving on.\n- If many students select incorrect answers (A, C, or D),\n- Revisit key slides that explain how bias comes from data, not just code.\n- Clarify that more data isn't always better—biased data will still lead to biased AI.\n- Have students answer the question again using a show of fingers before transitioning.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a751befa-3a76-4b7e-9ed9-8e68eca7c3ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc9b6c69-aa9e-4b6d-805e-6fa04bc91ac4",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**Students analyze bias in decision-making by comparing their own candidate selection process with AI’s and exploring how training data influences AI’s choices.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "AI Audit Challenges"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc9b6c69-aa9e-4b6d-805e-6fa04bc91ac4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "381a7b08-b59c-400d-8821-324aa3bf2fbf",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI is increasingly being used to make important decisions—hiring employees, admitting students, and even determining which patients receive medical care first. But how do we know if AI is making fair and unbiased choices? Today, you'll take on a decision-making role and compare your choices to AI's.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "381a7b08-b59c-400d-8821-324aa3bf2fbf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47e5f6e9-9806-4443-89dd-879ee8098aaa",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 and 4.\n- **Level 3:** Students choose one decision-making role and navigate an interactive panel level:\n   - **Hiring Manager:** Selects top candidates for job interviews.\n   - **College Admissions Officer:** Picks students for acceptance.\n   - **Hospital Triage Nurse:** Prioritizes patients for urgent care.\n- **Level 4:** Students review resources in Sketch Lab related to their scenario and rank their candidates. \n\nThey record and justify selections in both the activity guide\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Listen for students discussing why certain candidates stood out over others. If students struggle, ask:\n- *\"What specific qualifications made you choose these candidates?\"*\n- *\"Did you consider factors beyond qualifications, like personal background?\"*",
        "progression_name": "Decision-Making Roles"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47e5f6e9-9806-4443-89dd-879ee8098aaa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ae79a06-59f0-4662-9256-5abf22b92f0a",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ae79a06-59f0-4662-9256-5abf22b92f0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1b396f9-11e5-460c-ae6d-a910505b7f85",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, let's see how AI makes the same decision. AI doesn't have opinions or instincts—it relies on patterns in data. You'll ask AI to select candidates for your decision-making role, analyze its reasoning, and compare it to your own. Finally, you'll refine your prompt to see if AI's choices can better match your priorities.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1b396f9-11e5-460c-ae6d-a910505b7f85",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8f02a287-8b9c-4750-a595-04ee8358ac02",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 5 to complete Levels 5 and 6.\n- **Level 5:** Students will ask AI Chat to select candidates for the same decision-making role they previously chose. Students will then compare AI's selections to their own\n- **Level 6:** Student refine the the system prompt to better align with their priorities, specifying which qualities AI should prioritize while abstracting away irrelevant details.\n- **Level 7:** Students answer a quick question about careers in computer science.\n\n",
        "progression_name": "AI Audit Challenge"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8f02a287-8b9c-4750-a595-04ee8358ac02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3e24ddd-513a-4dce-86d1-a626e03f7d32",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Form student groups of three, ensuring each student has a different role, to share their findings.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *alignment* and share two mitigation strategies for reducing AI bias.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3e24ddd-513a-4dce-86d1-a626e03f7d32",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7f01d43-9d24-42e5-8f85-2c1825dea81c",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Besides the scenarios we just looked at, where else could biased AI cause harm?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students to identify real-world areas where biased AI could cause harm. Use this discussion to gauge students’ awareness of AI’s social impact beyond the examples discussed. If students are struggling to come up with examples, you can point to a few example applications like: housing, medicine, or policing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7f01d43-9d24-42e5-8f85-2c1825dea81c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e788f8e7-b705-453f-9bc1-6ccc7d866841"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2d9a54e3-dd14-4233-a2c6-dc28a76a31a6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a moment to think about the Question of the Day. Click the animated slide to reveal the 3-2-1 prompt. Have students share 3 examples of AI bias from this lesson, 2 things to help reduce AI bias, and 1 cause of AI bias. \n\n\n\n",
        "name": "3-2-1 Reflection and Discussion",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Teachers can choose to have students share the 3-2-1 prompt with a partner, call on students to share with the class, or have students write their responses on a sticky note and turn it in as an exit ticket."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2d9a54e3-dd14-4233-a2c6-dc28a76a31a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53f8518c-97b8-4638-98d6-c01a6314471f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5cfce1d0-01a1-4aa7-af24-1bbc0734f70e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5cfce1d0-01a1-4aa7-af24-1bbc0734f70e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53f8518c-97b8-4638-98d6-c01a6314471f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5e0dd3d-b845-4d21-9178-e441faf756d5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Student Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5e0dd3d-b845-4d21-9178-e441faf756d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53f8518c-97b8-4638-98d6-c01a6314471f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c2f7e5f-1c35-4757-a5ef-6bcd1df53733",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**Reveal how AI can generate misinformation by mimicking human knowledge gaps.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Place students in groups of four.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group 12-15 sticky notes. Each students needs at least three.\n",
        "name": "Hallucination Chain",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If needed, adjust the number of sticky notes based on group size so each student has three.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c2f7e5f-1c35-4757-a5ef-6bcd1df53733",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0dd807bf-234d-42b7-9732-49530cc16d65"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a016f269-cd19-4576-8030-5fd5b1924a87",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a016f269-cd19-4576-8030-5fd5b1924a87",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0dd807bf-234d-42b7-9732-49530cc16d65"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b656525a-2a80-44bc-8131-76f912b1458f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Have students each write three facts about the Great Wall of China on sticky notes - one fact per sticky note. Tell students that if they are unsure, they can make up something that sounds believable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are writing individual facts and not collaborating at this stage. If students hesitate, remind them that they can create facts if they’re unsure.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Have groups examine their sticky notes and categorize them as similarities, contradictions, or inaccuracies.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students who categorize everything as similar or accurate—prompt them to look for contradictions.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, prompt them with: \"How do we know if something is inaccurate?\" or \"What makes two facts similar?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b656525a-2a80-44bc-8131-76f912b1458f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0dd807bf-234d-42b7-9732-49530cc16d65"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b54b72a3-f0c4-42ef-84de-14f33f216ca4",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b54b72a3-f0c4-42ef-84de-14f33f216ca4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0dd807bf-234d-42b7-9732-49530cc16d65"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cded65ae-e9ff-4f4a-8b2b-409e6480dae8",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What might happen if we published these \"facts\" on a public website?*\n- *How does this activity mimic the way that AI generates responses?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize just like we created statements that sounded true but weren't, AI can hallucinate and generate misinformation that seems convincing. AI models learn from human input—if that input contains misinformation, AI can amplify and spread it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cded65ae-e9ff-4f4a-8b2b-409e6480dae8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0dd807bf-234d-42b7-9732-49530cc16d65"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b2997b3-3bbf-4591-9057-4ec958f6bd27",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**Show how AI hallucinations cause harm in real-world applications.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:**  Give each student a copy of the Hallucination Case Studies Activity Guide and a Jigsaw Article.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that students will read about real-world AI hallucinations in industries like law, travel, healthcare, and transportation. They will read independently first, then discuss their findings with their group.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that hallucinations exist because AI doesn’t have a database of “true” facts. It predicts based on patterns in data. If it is trained on a large set of data that haven’t been fact-checked, it can generate biased and inaccurate output.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Instruct students to read their assigned Jigsaw Article individually. Each student should annotate their article by highlighting key terms (e.g., AI Hallucination, accuracy, fairness, impact) and writing notes or questions in the margins. Students should also answer the reflection questions in their Hallucination Case Studies Activity Guide​ as they read their article.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Walk around and check that students are actively annotating and engaging with the text, jotting down notes in their activity guides. If students struggle, ask: \n- \"What was the AI hallucination in your article?\" \n- \"What was the real-world impact?\" \n- \"Would a human have made the same mistake?\"\n\nIf students are only highlighting without writing notes, encourage them to add margin notes or questions to deepen their analysis.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Hallucination Case Studies",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students finish early, have them jot down a real-world example where they've seen AI generate incorrect or misleading information (e.g., a chatbot making up a statistic, an AI-generated image mislabeling an object). This helps students connect their reading to prior knowledge and real-world experiences."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b2997b3-3bbf-4591-9057-4ec958f6bd27",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72da6ada-7a86-4d2c-ab93-112511b80c19"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64926ba6-06ec-4d38-b4a2-328e4fcdba86",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64926ba6-06ec-4d38-b4a2-328e4fcdba86",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72da6ada-7a86-4d2c-ab93-112511b80c19"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "815a543f-7ee5-437f-86a0-a234523ad931",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Have students share their article summaries with their group members. Each student should briefly explain what their article was about, what the AI hallucination was, and its consequences. After each student shares, the group discusses the real-world impact of AI hallucinations and what could have been done differently.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for common themes in student discussions. If students struggle to identify AI's impact, ask: \n- \"Who was affected by this AI mistake?\"\n- \"What industries could be impacted by similar hallucinations?\"\n- \"What steps could have been taken to avoid this hallucination?\"\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If groups struggle to connect their article’s AI hallucination to broader issues, encourage them to think about similar situations in other fields (e.g., misinformation in journalism, AI-generated legal errors, biased hiring algorithms)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "815a543f-7ee5-437f-86a0-a234523ad931",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72da6ada-7a86-4d2c-ab93-112511b80c19"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf742df1-358c-4091-b6e2-c44cb1c1ef3b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly explain that data centers, which are huge facilities full of computers, power AI tools. These computers use significant electricity, similar to the energy consumption of entire countries. As AI use expands, so does that demand.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Should this be part of how we judge AI?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that generative AI’s environmental impact through energy use and emissions—is an important factor to consider when evaluating AI systems and their real-world effects. Listen for students who:\n- Acknowledge trade-offs between AI’s benefits and its environmental costs\n- Recognize that small individual use scales up to large global impact\n- Connect environmental impact to fairness or equity issues\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf742df1-358c-4091-b6e2-c44cb1c1ef3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72da6ada-7a86-4d2c-ab93-112511b80c19"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89a7e8b9-5546-4ac4-953e-ac31e229c1f7",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**Students get practice with evaluating AI responses that contain hallucinations.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Reduce Hallucinations and Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89a7e8b9-5546-4ac4-953e-ac31e229c1f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72da6ada-7a86-4d2c-ab93-112511b80c19"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a794219c-4fad-4a40-84aa-67427c192116",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've seen the real-world impact of AI hallucinations, let's look at three strategies we can use to minimize misinformation when working with AI.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a794219c-4fad-4a40-84aa-67427c192116",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72da6ada-7a86-4d2c-ab93-112511b80c19"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1ed3c41-a5ed-495c-ab54-31502701fdfe",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain how it helps prevent AI misinformation. Use real-world examples where possible, such as AI misquoting sources or giving outdated information. Remind students that AI doesn't fact-check—it predicts likely responses based on past data.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to connect these strategies to their own experiences using AI tools or searching for information online. If students struggle to see the relevance, ask: \"What happens if you search for a fact and only look at the first result without verifying it?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1ed3c41-a5ed-495c-ab54-31502701fdfe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72da6ada-7a86-4d2c-ab93-112511b80c19"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36ed3ef1-8617-4951-9040-2855e435d33e",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36ed3ef1-8617-4951-9040-2855e435d33e",
