      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a0aad6a7-68d1-4bdf-92d8-1d84615d582b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 2.\n- **Level 1:** Students review how to reduce AI hallucinations.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a formative assessment.\n",
        "progression_name": "Reduce AI Hallucinations"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a0aad6a7-68d1-4bdf-92d8-1d84615d582b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72da6ada-7a86-4d2c-ab93-112511b80c19"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8f88e37-a120-4c0e-8ac9-2a9bb8c1af4d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8f88e37-a120-4c0e-8ac9-2a9bb8c1af4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72da6ada-7a86-4d2c-ab93-112511b80c19"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89ebcd56-c769-4413-a8e9-a459cc70c61f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe students as they complete the assessment. Look for signs that they understand AI hallucinations and how to counter them. Note if students struggle to connect fact-checking techniques to real-world examples.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses to assess how well they grasp the risks of AI hallucinations and the strategies to counter them. Teachers can determine next steps based on common student responses:\n- If students identify hallucinations but struggle to explain their impact:\n   - Have students revisit the Hallucination Case Studies slide and discuss their notes in small groups.\n   - Ask: \"Why does AI misinformation matter in real-world settings?\"\n- If students don't apply fact-checking strategies:\n   - Direct them to the Reduce AI Hallucinations slide and have them match each strategy (careful prompting, skepticism, fact-checking) to a real-world example.\n   - Ask: “Which strategy would help verify this AI-generated claim?”\n- If students effectively explain AI hallucinations, impacts, and fact-checking strategies:\n   - Move on to the wrap-up discussion.\n   - Have students share how they would explain AI hallucinations to someone unfamiliar with them.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89ebcd56-c769-4413-a8e9-a459cc70c61f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72da6ada-7a86-4d2c-ab93-112511b80c19"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "582823f9-b949-4afe-b75e-676f68637275",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**Engage students in critical thinking about AI hallucinations.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a moment to think about the Question of the Day before sharing with a partner. Encourage them to use specific examples from the lesson, such as case studies or AI-generated errors they encountered. Popcorn out the question with the class and get student perspectives.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The Question of the Day can written on an “exit ticket” and be used as a formative assessment to check whether students can recall real-world examples of AI hallucinations and their impacts. If students struggle to provide examples, review key moments from the lesson, such as the case studies and AI panel discussion.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "582823f9-b949-4afe-b75e-676f68637275",
        "lesson_activity.key": "697adf43-dac4-4a5b-ab7f-2c3c0f18c8ca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13c120f2-180a-4195-bf8e-247c94b7c446",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13c120f2-180a-4195-bf8e-247c94b7c446",
        "lesson_activity.key": "697adf43-dac4-4a5b-ab7f-2c3c0f18c8ca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88c5efe1-549f-4bd3-a1c9-11f0c1ae0b69",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that students will respond to statements about AI hallucinations by standing up if they agree or staying seated if they disagree. Emphasize that there are no right or wrong answers—the goal is to encourage reasoning and discussion.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read each statement aloud. Pause after each statement and let students vote by standing or sitting. Ask a standing student and a seated student to share their reasoning. Use the follow-up prompts:\n- *What makes hallucinations easy or hard to spot?*\n- *Can you think of a real-world example where an AI hallucination was helpful or harmful?*\n- *What factors make AI hallucinations dangerous in some cases but not others?*\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students all agree on a statement, challenge them by asking: \"Can you argue the other side?\" or \"What might change your perspective?\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88c5efe1-549f-4bd3-a1c9-11f0c1ae0b69",
        "lesson_activity.key": "697adf43-dac4-4a5b-ab7f-2c3c0f18c8ca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78c5f7e7-3884-42d8-b8da-e7049ed1f0b3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78c5f7e7-3884-42d8-b8da-e7049ed1f0b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "697adf43-dac4-4a5b-ab7f-2c3c0f18c8ca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f8e4918-d8ad-4b13-88d9-7e22b206beef",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f8e4918-d8ad-4b13-88d9-7e22b206beef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "697adf43-dac4-4a5b-ab7f-2c3c0f18c8ca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "073680f4-f838-4f79-8729-dc7bc294b2c1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Introduce prompt engineering as a valuable skill, and clarify that the job title itself faded even as the underlying skill spread into many other roles.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"AI's Hottest New Job Pays Up to $250K a Year. So I Applied\" video.\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:**\n- *Where do you think this skill shows up now?*\n\n**Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that \"prompt engineer\" was the hot titled job when this video was made, but the standalone title has since mostly disappeared. The skill itself — giving clear, effective instructions to get useful results from AI — didn't go away; it got absorbed into many other roles. Students name at least one job or field where they think this skill shows up today (e.g., customer support, marketing, software development, research).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *prompt engineering*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "073680f4-f838-4f79-8729-dc7bc294b2c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6bf4932-bbe4-4ffd-8ccf-b225dd314ad8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc72a01a-c64e-4719-b333-6ca7dd16fbc7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover, through hands-on comparison, that small changes to a prompt's format and tone — not the underlying task — are what shape an AI's response, setting up the TaCoFoRT framework before it's formally introduced.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students in groups of two.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair one Prompt Showdown activity guide. Have partners split it in half so each student has their own half-sheet Prompt Showdown copy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct partners to complete Steps 1–3 on their Activity Guide — picking a shared topic and task, then each partner picking a different format and tone, then each combining their own into a prompt.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for pairs who accidentally pick the same format or tone — remind them each partner's combination needs to be different. If a partner is stuck combining their pieces into one prompt, ask: \"What's your topic and task? Now say it in your format, in your tone.\" Don't give them a finished example prompt to copy — the point is for them to write their own.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Prompt Showdown",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This step should move fast. Aim for about 1 minute on Step 1, 1 minute on Step 2, and 2 minutes for Step 3 — set a visible timer if that helps pairs keep pace. Leaving Step 3 open-ended is intentional, but if a pair is still blank after a minute, prompt them verbally rather than letting them stall on paper.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc72a01a-c64e-4719-b333-6ca7dd16fbc7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d02b2fb3-26da-48cc-a5f8-33bbe07af5b1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d02b2fb3-26da-48cc-a5f8-33bbe07af5b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d586811-b07f-4801-a584-2fff3b8594db",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**  Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1 to type the prompt they wrote in Step 3 into AI Chat and see the response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Confirm each student is testing their own prompt, not their partner's. If a pair finishes early, have them start comparing responses out loud before the whole-class discussion.\n",
        "progression_name": "Prompt Showdown"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d586811-b07f-4801-a584-2fff3b8594db",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1af39e00-7e8f-4399-ade8-d1aa13a9451c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *What's different about your two outputs?*\n* *You started with the same task — so what caused the difference?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the task and topic were identical, but the format and tone they each chose changed the AI's response. The specific details students add to a prompt — not just the task itself — shape what they get back.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1af39e00-7e8f-4399-ade8-d1aa13a9451c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5eda2ee5-0b74-4e7c-95fb-df7b8580a5ca",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What you two just did on paper has a name — it's called TaCoFoRT. You picked a Task and topic together, then each of you chose a different Format and Tone. There are two more pieces — Context and Retrieval — that we didn't use on paper, but you'll use both in the next activity.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5eda2ee5-0b74-4e7c-95fb-df7b8580a5ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f594c93-b215-4488-b1fe-8c1994b543fb",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Introduce the five elements of a strong prompt — Task, Context, Format, Retrieval, and Tone — as a framework students apply together to build one complete system prompt for a real purpose.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to introduce the key elements of a prompt. Explain that just like giving instructions to a friend, the more details you provide, the better the response. Task tells the AI what to do, Context tells it the background of the situation or purpose, Format shapes how the answer looks, Retrieval gives it real information to draw from, and Tone sets the style.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide one bubble at a time — TASK, CONTEXT, FORMAT, RETRIEVAL, TONE. For each one, ask students which part of the prompt they think matches, then click to reveal the corresponding section that's highlighted. Once all five are revealed, ask students if anything seems to be missing from the prompt, and take a few guesses before continuing.\n",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "AI Prompting Framework"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f594c93-b215-4488-b1fe-8c1994b543fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db369b7d-ba0d-43cb-9c60-823a0a14a420",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "All five elements are here — but when building an AI chat agent, the system prompt should include one more thing: a line that tells the AI chatbot agent to stay on topic. Something like — 'Only answer questions about the food truck. If someone asks something unrelated, politely redirect them back to Rosa's Tacos.' This isn't one of our five TaCoFoRT elements, but any AI Chat agent created needs it, and you'll add one to your own AI chatbot agent in the next activity.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db369b7d-ba0d-43cb-9c60-823a0a14a420",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd4c2d0c-7c07-4ad2-8e6a-180ae07add9e",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Revisit \"TaCo FoRT\" as a memory device: Task, Context, Format, Retrieval, Tone. Revisit \"TaCo FoRT\" as a memory device: Task, Context, Format, Retrieval, Tone.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Suggest that students use \"TaCoFoRT\" to systematically check if they've included each element in their prompts. This prompting framework can be used in future interactions with AI Chatbots in this lesson and beyond.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd4c2d0c-7c07-4ad2-8e6a-180ae07add9e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a79afe0-d6bc-4c5e-96d3-3dbf258c44df",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that refining a prompt is like debugging code — you test it, see what happens, fix what's not working, and test again. Tell students that in the next activity, they test their AI chatbot agent three ways: something it should know, something it doesn't, and something totally unrelated. Whatever breaks is what they'll fix.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Career Spec Sheet to use while building their AI chatbot agent.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a79afe0-d6bc-4c5e-96d3-3dbf258c44df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1990822-fb7a-493b-ab00-779120bfb434",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 3. \n* **Level 2:** Students choose one of four businesses, write a TaCoFoRT system prompt, then test and debug based on what they find.\n* **Level 3:** Students answer a check for understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for prompts missing one of the five TaCoFoRT elements, especially if the Retrieval is left empty or the stay-on-topic line skipped entirely. If a student's AI chatbot agent answers an off-topic question anyway, that's a good moment to point them back to their own prompt rather than fixing it for them. Watch also for students copying facts word-for-word from the Career Spec Sheet without deciding which ones their AI chatbot agent actually needs.\n",
        "progression_name": "Build Your TaCoFoRT Prompt",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 2 and 3 can be used to formatively assess the lesson objectives. Look for a system prompt that includes all five TaCoFoRT elements plus a stay-on-topic line, Retrieval cards drawn from the Career Spec Sheet, and a Level 3 response that names a real problem found during testing and the specific change made to fix it — not just \"it worked fine.\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1990822-fb7a-493b-ab00-779120bfb434",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc7ae891-c4a4-47d8-8220-d96e6cdc50ba",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *What problem did you find when you tested your AI chatbot agent?*\n* *How did you fix it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Different businesses will surface different bugs — a missing Retrieval fact, an off-topic question the AI chatbot agent didn't decline, a tone that didn't fit. Students should recognize that testing reveals real gaps, and that fixing a prompt or Retrieval card and testing again is the same debugging loop used in software development. Adding Retrieval information reduces hallucinations and vague responses — the core takeaway is that AI outputs improve through testing and iteration, not by getting the prompt right on the first try.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc7ae891-c4a4-47d8-8220-d96e6cdc50ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "74c4cf83-e32e-4d78-86a3-3c1dfb5cc8b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "261bf95b-c03d-4fb4-866f-fa898afb093b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students finish the sentence \"You can improve AI's responses by ___\" using something they learned from building or testing their own AI chatbot agent.\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, have students write their response on a sticky note and use the next three slides to have them add it to the classroom anchor chart. If time is tight, have students think of their answer, share it with a partner, then cold-call one or two pairs to share with the class.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "261bf95b-c03d-4fb4-866f-fa898afb093b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5ee1062-3384-4d9a-81e3-5e9b593a07b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6ec8eba-5d40-4d34-8b50-08bfbbed3fc0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this OPTIONAL activity:** Students summarize their learning about AI prompt refinement in a concise, creative, and engaging way.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a sticky note.\n\n",
        "name": "[Optional] Key Elements Anchor Chart",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This slide and the next two are only needed if you chose the sticky-note path on the previous slide. If you did a think-pair-share instead, skip ahead to the next section.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6ec8eba-5d40-4d34-8b50-08bfbbed3fc0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5ee1062-3384-4d9a-81e3-5e9b593a07b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b34ac72-4853-4a3d-9e5b-b9d35eb19ef8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students write their Question of the Day sentence on their sticky note, then bring it up to add to the classroom anchor chart.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Prompt deeper thinking with a guiding question: What did testing your bot reveal? What specifically did you change after something didn't work?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students add their sticky notes to the classroom anchor chart.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use color-coded notes for different key elements to create a more structured visual representation. This classroom anchor chart will become useful for students to try new ways to refine their prompts throughout this unit and others in the AIF curriculum. Encourage students to add to the anchor chart whenever they have a new way of refining their prompts that leads to more interesting, accurate, and fair output.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The Question of the Day can be used as a formative check of whether students connect testing and debugging to improved AI outputs. Make sure students write their name on their sticky note if you plan to use it as an assessment.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b34ac72-4853-4a3d-9e5b-b9d35eb19ef8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5ee1062-3384-4d9a-81e3-5e9b593a07b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e47b9748-afc7-48cb-a60a-acc3de54bf9d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e47b9748-afc7-48cb-a60a-acc3de54bf9d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f5ee1062-3384-4d9a-81e3-5e9b593a07b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "883685a6-ac26-45b1-8d1e-00f7bedbef9e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that AI-generated music is difficult to distinguish from human-made music. This plants the lesson's central question — if you can't tell the difference, what makes music \"creative\" and who owns it? — without answering it yet.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Play each audio clip. After both clips, students vote by a show of hands: which was made by a person? Call on one or two students to share what they listened for, then reveal the answer (Audio #1 is AI, Audio #2 is human). There is no wrong answer here — the class splitting or guessing wrong is the point. If that happens, name it: \"If we can't reliably tell, does it matter which is which?\" Keep this brief; the lesson returns to this question all day.\n",
        "progression_name": "AI in Music Lab",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Both clips are very short — feel free to replay them as needed before students vote.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "883685a6-ac26-45b1-8d1e-00f7bedbef9e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c5697b2a-1de4-4f57-8766-1a20765637dd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59a1ec7f-97d3-4fe2-8e98-a30e86c01186",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59a1ec7f-97d3-4fe2-8e98-a30e86c01186",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c5697b2a-1de4-4f57-8766-1a20765637dd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c96de6f-acff-40f5-b80d-0412d98b5c07",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c96de6f-acff-40f5-b80d-0412d98b5c07",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c5697b2a-1de4-4f57-8766-1a20765637dd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a811d9a-355f-42cd-8417-6835c324c20d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i>**Goal of this activity:** Students explore AI Music Lab through discovery, then apply it to compose a short theme, building the skills they will use again in the Product Launch project. Along the way, they experience firsthand what the AI contributes and what still requires their creativity.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Explore and Create "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a811d9a-355f-42cd-8417-6835c324c20d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91667405-9262-4b65-9886-1f9d3522035f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1eb814f-54a9-4790-b4b7-cc62c5b2406e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1 to explore AI Music Lab freely for about three minutes. Students click into the play AI drums block, add beats to the grid, and discover what the Generate button does. Encourage experimenting with the Control and Sounds menus — there is no task to complete on this level.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students who haven't found the Generate button (it appears after four beats are placed on the grid) — prompt with \"What happens if you add a few more beats?\" rather than pointing directly. Note interesting discoveries you overhear; you can call on those students during the Notice/Wonder debrief.\n",
        "progression_name": "Explore Music Lab",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Resist the urge to demo the tool first. The next slide's debrief works best when discoveries come from students — your job during these three minutes is to collect who found what.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1eb814f-54a9-4790-b4b7-cc62c5b2406e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91667405-9262-4b65-9886-1f9d3522035f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd7d72c0-6dd3-4288-a832-27b3e93bfc92",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *What did you notice about Music Lab?*\n* *What do you wonder about Music Lab?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students surface the features they need for the next two levels. Take several responses for each prompt, using discoveries collected while circulating to call on specific students. As features come up, name them for the class so everyone leaves with shared vocabulary. **Important:** before moving on, make sure these four moves have been named — seed any students didn't mention:\n* Generate appears after four beats and the AI continues your pattern\n* Clicking Generate again produces a different variation from the same start\n* The AI's output can be edited after generating\n* Adding additional Sounds or Control blocks to the program \n\n\"Wonder\" responses about how the AI decides what to add do not need answers yet — collect them and say the class returns to that question shortly.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd7d72c0-6dd3-4288-a832-27b3e93bfc92",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91667405-9262-4b65-9886-1f9d3522035f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b2664df-e8a3-4f57-bb4d-c260ac1af86e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n* **Level 2:** Students choose a real track and build a short mix by sampling pieces of the song.\n* **Level 3:** Students compose a four-beat theme for a movie scene or game moment.\n* **Level 4:** Students answer Check for Understanding questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** The track list is the biggest time risk — redirect students still browsing songs after a minute with \"Pick one; you can sample any track in the project later.\" On Level 3, listen for whether students are choosing sounds for their scene's mood or stacking blocks at random; prompt with \"What is your scene? What should it feel like?\" Students finish Level 3 at different times — the Level 4 questions are self-paced and absorb the difference, so no one should be waiting.\n",
        "progression_name": "Sample and Create",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 3 and 4 provide formative evidence for the lesson objectives:\n* Level 3 (theme project) — Evaluate the strengths and limitations of generative AI: look for students directing the AI toward their scene's mood — generating, rejecting, regenerating, and editing output — rather than accepting the first result. Deliberate revision is the evidence; a first-try submission suggests the student hasn't yet engaged with what the AI does and doesn't do well.\n* Level 4, Questions 1–2 — Evaluate strengths and limitations and analyze the role of human creativity: strong responses name that the AI predicts from patterns in existing music (Q1) and identify something specific the student contributed that the AI could not — choosing the scene, judging the mood, deciding what to keep (Q2). Watch for answers crediting the AI with intent or emotion (\"it knew what would sound good\") — address these during the \"How Does AI Generate Music?\" debrief on an upcoming slide.\n* Level 4, Question 3 is a stance commitment for the debate, not an assessment item — there is no correct answer. The ownership objective is assessed after the debate, in the Wrap Up.\n\nUse what you see to adjust the upcoming debrief: if many responses credit the AI with intent, spend an extra beat on the \"patterns, not feelings\" point.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b2664df-e8a3-4f57-bb4d-c260ac1af86e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91667405-9262-4b65-9886-1f9d3522035f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25dd5830-74ee-4541-b42c-78a29aad5c68",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**  Students connect what they just experienced in Music Lab to how generative AI actually works, then meet the real careers and licensing questions behind music — setting up the debate.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Before revealing any bullets, pose the title question and take one or two guesses — students have seen how AI learns from patterns in training data earlier in this unit. Then click through the bullets, tying each to what students just did: predicting from patterns is why Generate gave different results from the same start, and why the AI never asked what mood the scene needed — that was the student's job. If Level 4 answers credited the AI with intent, correct it here: the AI finds the most probable next sound; it does not feel or intend. Land on the final bullet: those patterns came from real artists' music — like the tracks students just sampled.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Debate Setup"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25dd5830-74ee-4541-b42c-78a29aad5c68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91667405-9262-4b65-9886-1f9d3522035f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c48765bd-37a5-4345-8cac-0d8e37c83761",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The AI learned from real artists' music — and you just sampled a real artist's track yourself. So who should get credit for what you made? Before we debate this, let's meet some people whose careers depend on the answer.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c48765bd-37a5-4345-8cac-0d8e37c83761",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91667405-9262-4b65-9886-1f9d3522035f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55a1d44e-6b46-4834-b5f4-c4f80bf99354",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:**Show the \"Meet Henny the Bizness, Music Producer\" video. Make sure to stop the video at 1:33 when he says \"... small mobile devices to get really good output.\"\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:**\n- What does a music producer do that the AI you just used can't?\n- Henny says good ideas come from what \"feels good\" and \"gives you chills.\" Can an AI use that test?\n\n\n**Discussion Goal:** Students should name the human parts of Henny's process — reading the room, deciding what feels right, judging which ideas are worth keeping. Henny closes the segment saying technology keeps making music creation more accessible; pose the pivot: Is AI just the next step in that story — or something different? Take one or two responses, then move on — the debate is where this argument belongs, so do not resolve it here.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55a1d44e-6b46-4834-b5f4-c4f80bf99354",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91667405-9262-4b65-9886-1f9d3522035f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ce7a21e-711f-41d3-850f-64ca3c6d4b62",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**  Students construct and defend positions on whether AI should be used to create music and art, and recognize that creativity and ownership of AI-generated content are genuinely contested questions with defensible positions on multiple sides. This is where the ownership objective is taught.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Put students into groups of four.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group one set of Debate Perspective Cards (one card per person). Briefly name the four roles as cards go out — each is a real career with something different at stake when AI makes music. When naming the lawyer, define licensing in one line: getting legal permission to use someone's music — it's how artists get paid when their work gets used. The tracks in Music Lab are licensed — that permission is why students could sample them. The debate is about the harder question: whether AI tools in general should need that same permission for the music they learn from.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Table Debates",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If groups of four don't work out evenly: in a group of three, set aside the streaming executive card — the musician, hip-hop artist, and lawyer create the strongest three-way tension. In a group of five, two students share the musician card.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ce7a21e-711f-41d3-850f-64ca3c6d4b62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91667405-9262-4b65-9886-1f9d3522035f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e2a06e2b-63b7-491b-b9e3-adf449186805",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the debate question and walk through the Round 1 structure. Give students a couple of minutes to read their card and build their argument, jotting notes on the back of their card — the position they take is theirs to construct; the card gives them who they are, what's at stake, and one question their person would raise. Then start the table debates, protecting at least five to six minutes of debate time. Every student speaks; their job is to make sure their card's perspective is heard at the table, not to win.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Listen for students asserting positions without reasons — prompt with \"What would your character say back to that?\" If a table agrees too quickly, seed the counterargument: \"What would the lawyer say about who gets paid?\" Note one or two strong arguments you overhear to pull into the synthesis discussion.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students do not need to agree with their card — Round 1 is about making sure all four perspectives get a fair hearing before Round 2, where students argue their own views. If a student resists their card's stance, that is fine: representing a view is not endorsing it, and their turn to speak for themselves is two minutes away.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Collect the cards after the debate if you want written evidence of the ownership objective — the back of each card shows whether the student built an argument with reasons or just restated the card's stance. \n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e2a06e2b-63b7-491b-b9e3-adf449186805",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91667405-9262-4b65-9886-1f9d3522035f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc9a13e0-f0ca-429e-8d26-d8328d70712b",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to put their cards face-down — Round 2 is their own views. Tables debate the same question again, but each student argues what they actually think. Hold them to the one rule on the slide: their position has to respond to at least one perspective from Round 1 — agreeing with it, refuting it, or complicating it. Students who committed a stance in the Check for Understanding can start there — and it is fair game to have changed their mind since. Give this round about four to five minutes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  This round runs hotter than Round 1 — that is the point, so let it. Redirect only when a table drifts off the question or an argument stops engaging the perspectives (\"that's your opinion — but what would you say to the musician?\"). Keep collecting strong arguments for the synthesis discussion.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If a table's Round 2 sounds identical to its Round 1, the students may be hiding behind their cards. Prompt: \"Forget your card — you, personally: would you put an AI-made song on your playlist? Why?\" A concrete personal question usually breaks the seal.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc9a13e0-f0ca-429e-8d26-d8328d70712b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91667405-9262-4b65-9886-1f9d3522035f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eaefeea1-05e2-46f4-8b61-98273cb3c1fa",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Before posing the question, spend about a minute harvesting the debate: ask \"What was the strongest argument you heard at your table — even one you disagreed with?\" and take one or two responses, using strong arguments collected while circulating (\"Table 3, share the one about who gets paid\").\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Question of the Day. Take a few responses, encouraging students to use an argument from the debate — including one they just heard — to support their thinking.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that the next few slides present statements about AI and creativity, connected to the debate students just had. For each one, students hold up a thumbs up if they agree, thumbs down if they disagree, or thumbs sideways if they're unsure. After each vote, call on one or two students from different positions (e.g., one thumbs up, one thumbs down) to explain their thinking.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display statement #1: AI is just as creative as humans. Students respond with a thumbs up, down, or sideways. Call on one student who agreed and one who disagreed to explain their thinking; invite a third if someone voted sideways to explain what made them unsure. Push for reasons grounded in today's experience — the warm-up guesses, how the AI actually generates music, Henny's process, or the debate — rather than general impressions.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, have students explain their choice to a partner first before opening the discussion to the whole class. This helps more students clarify their thinking and increases participation in the full-class share-out.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eaefeea1-05e2-46f4-8b61-98273cb3c1fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9b5978b3-fc2d-43d9-a182-0c34e143ff17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85049e62-4963-485f-bfa5-902598ca3277",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85049e62-4963-485f-bfa5-902598ca3277",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9b5978b3-fc2d-43d9-a182-0c34e143ff17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75314002-f6c1-45c9-8a94-6717d53b7719",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display statement #2: I would use AI again for a creative project. Students respond with a thumbs up, down, or sideways, then one student from each position briefly explains their reasoning. Push past \"it was fun\" or \"it was annoying\" — ask what specifically the AI did well or poorly in their theme, and what they had to do themselves. Students later use Music Lab again in the Product Launch project, so thumbs-down responses are worth hearing out: what would make AI worth using next time?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, have students explain their choice to a partner first. This gives everyone a chance to process and prepare their reasoning before the whole-class share-out.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75314002-f6c1-45c9-8a94-6717d53b7719",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9b5978b3-fc2d-43d9-a182-0c34e143ff17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c0d2d8a6-ca86-4ce4-ac95-9d5320ac478d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c0d2d8a6-ca86-4ce4-ac95-9d5320ac478d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9b5978b3-fc2d-43d9-a182-0c34e143ff17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ec1bcb8-ebe1-4756-95c5-883a6538b38b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display statement #3: The artists whose music trained the AI deserve credit when it makes something new. Students vote using thumbs up, down, or sideways, then one student from each position shares their reasoning. Push for reasons anchored in the debate — this is the question the musician, hip-hop artist, and lawyer cards argued from different sides. If students land on \"it depends,\" ask on what: how much the AI borrowed? Whether the artists gave permission? Whether anyone can even tell? Those distinctions are exactly the unsettled questions the law is still working out — an \"it depends\" with reasons is a strong answer here.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, have students explain their choice to a partner first. This gives everyone a chance to think through their answer before sharing aloud. Encourage students to reference their debate arguments or their Level 4 stance to support their reasoning.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ec1bcb8-ebe1-4756-95c5-883a6538b38b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9b5978b3-fc2d-43d9-a182-0c34e143ff17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c57dedf-eddd-4eb1-8671-98a649c9d0ca",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Give students a shared, low-stakes rehearsal of the hope/fear thinking they'll use on their own vision boards later in the lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario about Newsclues.com, a fictional website that is deciding to replace the people who pick the articles to feature on the site with an AI-powered news filtering system.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts. Have students think for 30 seconds, then share with a partner before inviting 2–3 responses\n- *What's your hope for a system like this? What could it do better than the team of 10?*\n- *What's your fear? What could go wrong — and who gets hurt?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The goal of this discussion is to help students critically evaluate the benefits and risks of AI decision-making in media curation. This discussion will set the stage for deeper exploration of AI's role in their own communities. Here are things to listen for:\n- AI is not neutral; it reflects biases in training data.\n- AI may favor certain perspectives while excluding others.\n- Risk of misinformation (e.g., promoting unreliable sources).\n- AI may prioritize engagement over accuracy.\n- Benefits: Companies (cost savings), increased efficiency.\n- Harms: Journalists (job loss), misinformation in communities.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Pacing**: Keep these discussions moving along and once you hear the points mentioned in the Discussion Goal, proceed to the next prompt or section of the lesson. If students have more to share than time allows, let them know that they will have a chance to share their voice later in the lesson as part of the main activity where they write an elevator pitch about AI use."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c57dedf-eddd-4eb1-8671-98a649c9d0ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d9a1889c-9c99-4534-8530-01a5d16ba551"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8fde2bd8-6900-4135-80f9-81ce7feb17ee",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8fde2bd8-6900-4135-80f9-81ce7feb17ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d9a1889c-9c99-4534-8530-01a5d16ba551"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a68c771-434d-4d70-bb76-ca83e55ead03",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students select a topic of interest that illustrates AI's role in society, both real and imagined. An AI Chat level supports their research by acting as a summarizing and fact-checking collaborator.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Levels 1 & 2:\n* **Level 1**: Students choose one role of AI in the community to focus on. Each choice sends them to a folder with three articles — a feature article, an editorial, and a tabloid-style article. Keep this quick (about 2 minutes): they are skimming headlines and first sentences to pick a topic that interests them, not reading deeply yet. \n* **Level 2**: Students attach articles related to the subject they chose and interrogate AI Chat about them\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Keep students moving toward a choice. Check in with:\n* \"Which of these AI uses have you actually run into yourself?\"\n* \"Which topic do you already have feelings about?\"\n* \"Which claim from the article didn't survive a fact-check?\" \n\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "AI's Role in Society ",
        "progression_name": "AI's Role in Society",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "These articles were written by CodeAI staff members, crafted along with ChatGPT / Gemini to synthesize around 20 different news articles on each subject. The facts presented all come from one or more of these news sources. You may choose to tailor the topic list to issues relevant in your students' community if you have sources they can use.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a68c771-434d-4d70-bb76-ca83e55ead03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaeba70a-6415-450f-bff8-5b4113630ecc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a8c0ebf4-031e-41ae-b999-d86f5bb6e172",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  What was one way that AI Chat assisted you with gathering information from your articles?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students name a concrete way AI Chat functioned as a research collaborator whether summarizing across articles, pulling key points, fact-checking a tabloid claim, or answering a follow-up question. Listen for students who pushed back or asked follow-ups when a summary was incomplete; that's the behavior to praise aloud. If answers are generic (\"it summarized\"), push once: \"What's one thing you learned from AI that you wouldn't have caught skimming on your own?\" This also quietly previews the second AI collaboration coming later: turning their board into a power phrase.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a8c0ebf4-031e-41ae-b999-d86f5bb6e172",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaeba70a-6415-450f-bff8-5b4113630ecc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d9dfd9c4-58cd-4179-bd96-89a5e51f6f8c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students investigate how AI is impacting their communities and build a vision board expressing their own hopes, fears, questions, and role, turning research into a personal point of view. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the concept of a vision board. Students may know vision boards from social media or goal-setting. Connect it to the Question of the Day: today's question is how AI should be used in solving real-world problems. This often depends on what people value and what they're willing to speak up about. \n",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "AI's Role - Vision Board  ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students will hear \"vision board\" and think decoration. Set the expectation that the best boards aren't the prettiest, they're the most specific. "
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d9dfd9c4-58cd-4179-bd96-89a5e51f6f8c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaeba70a-6415-450f-bff8-5b4113630ecc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cbc4cce-faea-4c2b-b810-754365d51297",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 3 to explore the vision board that they will be creating today. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students explore, make sure everyone has found the palette (icons, shapes, sticky notes), the model board, and the sentence starters. Check in with:\n* \"Which section do you think will be easiest for you? Which will be hardest?\"\n* \"What's one thing on the model board you wouldn't have thought of?\" The check-ins should confirm every student can navigate the canvas before the discussion — the next slide's notice/wonder works best when everyone has actually poked around.\n",
        "progression_name": "Build Your Vision Board",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Explain that students will be adding to this board using what they found in their articles and AI Chat research. Every idea worth keeping becomes a sticky note, image, or icon. They can also bring in outside images or ideas separate from the articles if they desire. By the end of class, the board shows what they hope, what they fear, what they still wonder, and what role they want to play."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cbc4cce-faea-4c2b-b810-754365d51297",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaeba70a-6415-450f-bff8-5b4113630ecc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4454af1-428e-4086-9c4a-834814022056",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What do you notice and what do you wonder about the vision board and model?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Two things might surface. First, every image on the model board has words attached (a label, a caption, a sticky note). Labeled ideas are clearer to viewers. Second, the model is specific — \"AI uses SO much energy?\" and \"Future AI Auditor\". Students should aim to say more than \"AI is bad\" or \"AI is cool\". Students might share wonders about how to fill their own board (\"Can I use just pictures?\" \"What goes in My Role?\").\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4454af1-428e-4086-9c4a-834814022056",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaeba70a-6415-450f-bff8-5b4113630ecc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "91c513b1-e1f1-45ff-b958-95fd086ddc84",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Students will now populate their vision boards using information from your articles, any outside sources / images, and the provided template in Sketch Lab.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students brainstorm an idea, encourage them to focus on the core problem or concern they uncovered in their research. Ask questions like: \n* What’s something you think should be done differently with this technology?\n* Who is being left out or harmed in the way this AI is currently used?\n* What would make this system more fair or useful for your school or neighborhood? \n\nSupport students in thinking practically and specifically — their ideas don’t need to be perfect or large-scale, just thoughtful and connected to what they learned.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "91c513b1-e1f1-45ff-b958-95fd086ddc84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaeba70a-6415-450f-bff8-5b4113630ecc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25ff2300-fdbe-4e17-b651-675cbcb82c02",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The board is your evidence of student thinking. As you circulate, look for: grounded fears (traceable to an article or fact-check, not \"AI takes over\"), action-oriented roles (something they'll do or ask, not a feeling), and real questions (things the articles genuinely didn't answer). "
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25ff2300-fdbe-4e17-b651-675cbcb82c02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaeba70a-6415-450f-bff8-5b4113630ecc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c94346d2-72d3-4369-a7cc-107ba1d87829",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students save their Vision Board sketch to the Backpack.  They will click the backpack icon on the left of the screen and then click the \"Save Sketch to Backpack\" button. Their canvas will export as a PNG that can be imported into AI Chat in the next level."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c94346d2-72d3-4369-a7cc-107ba1d87829",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaeba70a-6415-450f-bff8-5b4113630ecc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ebccc8c-eea8-4e2c-9597-1ef85322e81a",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 4. Students share their board with AI Chat and ask it to generate power phrases: 1–2 sentence summaries of what their board expresses. Frame the collaboration explicitly: \"You spent all class building that board, now let's have AI summarize what it sees. Your job is to judge whether it got you right.\" Students pick the strongest phrase and revise it. This phrase is what they'll share in the Give One, Get One activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for critical evaluation of the AI output:\n* \"Does that phrase sound like you, or like anybody?\"\n* \"What did AI get right about your board? What did it miss?\"\n* \"Which word would you swap to make it yours?\"\n",
        "progression_name": "Crafting a Power Phrase"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ebccc8c-eea8-4e2c-9597-1ef85322e81a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaeba70a-6415-450f-bff8-5b4113630ecc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d311423-4751-4f3c-b1e4-0a5d7c08ea1c",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that students will participate in a \"Give One, Get One\" exchange. They find a partner and take turns sharing their power phrases. One person gives their phrase, the other listens and gives quick feedback or asks a question. Then they switch, find new partners, and repeat. Encourage students to share with multiple classmates to hear a range of hopes, fears, and roles across different AI topics.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Play the music clip to signal the start of the activity. Use the same music cue consistently so students recognize when it's time to begin “Give One, Get One.”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students exchange phrases, listen for whether each phrase communicates something specific from the speaker's board. Prompt partners with: \n* \"What did your partner's phrase make you curious about?\"\n* \"Can you guess what's on their board from the phrase alone?\"\n* \"Was anything unclear or missing?\"\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Give One, Get One encourages students to seek and share ideas. Using music to cue transitions helps create classroom routines — when students hear the cue, they know it's time to circulate. Click the play icon on the slide to start the music. If you're short on time, two exchanges beat zero: even one round gets every voice in the room heard."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d311423-4751-4f3c-b1e4-0a5d7c08ea1c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaeba70a-6415-450f-bff8-5b4113630ecc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92996370-9de1-4dc5-b4b2-fe548fc3d5c7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students form small groups of three or four.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that the \"Pass the Mic\" discussion is designed so that everyone gets a chance to share. Each person answers the Question of the Day in one or two sentences and then \"passes the mic\" so everyone has a turn.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students share in their groups, listen for answers grounded in today's work rather than general opinions about AI. Prompt deeper thinking with:\n* \"What's on your board that wasn't in your head 40 minutes ago?\"\n* \"Did anyone's power phrase change how you think about your own topic?\"\n* \"You researched different topics — did you hear a hope or fear that matches yours?\"\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, close with a quick whole-class beat: hands up if you heard a perspective in your group that made you think differently. If a student only offers a general opinion (\"AI is helpful\"), follow up with: \"What did you find today that backs that up or complicates it?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92996370-9de1-4dc5-b4b2-fe548fc3d5c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8287c67a-990a-4383-b68f-985e9257e825"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "199a86e9-117f-4660-87d5-99a49ea2719a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  Click through the animated slide to display the prompts:\n- *If a friend wanted to locate a certain bird in the wild, how would you help them? Would you give them . . .*\n- *a written description?*\n- *a picture?*\n- *a video?*\n- *an audio recording of its call?*\n- *something else?*\n- *Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that different types of media serve different purposes. Text provides details, images help with identification, and audio and video capture sound and movement. AI must process different inputs to generate useful responses.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to pick a media type, prompt them with follow up questions, such as: \"How do YOU recognize a bird in the wild?\" or \"Would text be enough to help a friend who has never seen this bird before?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "199a86e9-117f-4660-87d5-99a49ea2719a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e767aeb2-8850-441c-bea1-7ff98f729db7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "400055d7-867d-4b46-94f8-66f26f21345a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "400055d7-867d-4b46-94f8-66f26f21345a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e767aeb2-8850-441c-bea1-7ff98f729db7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8cb379b0-cb90-416c-82b2-86356de90306",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI models like GPT-4, that powers ChatGPT, are trained to analyze multiple types of input (text, images, audio). Some AI models input text only, some input text and images, some input video, etc. Our AI Chat allows us to input text, images, and PDFs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8cb379b0-cb90-416c-82b2-86356de90306",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e767aeb2-8850-441c-bea1-7ff98f729db7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "94738092-41a1-4b56-bece-7169bfaeeab2",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *multimodal model*. Provide students with some real-world examples, such as Google Lens, self-driving cars, ChatGPT's image recognition, etc.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students ask about AI that uses all four types of input (text, image, video, and sound), acknowledge that some AI models specialize in one type while others combine them. Self-driving cars, for example, use vision and audio sensors."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "94738092-41a1-4b56-bece-7169bfaeeab2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e767aeb2-8850-441c-bea1-7ff98f729db7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c228dfe9-d083-4260-8a17-8284f1b43d8d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c228dfe9-d083-4260-8a17-8284f1b43d8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e767aeb2-8850-441c-bea1-7ff98f729db7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b7952066-ebc5-466f-a0fe-3188e3022313",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Multimodal AI isn't just used in chatbots – it's changing industries.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b7952066-ebc5-466f-a0fe-3188e3022313",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e767aeb2-8850-441c-bea1-7ff98f729db7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fcac538-3c7c-4864-8057-252f0c60805f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the examples on the slide to show how AI uses multiple inputs in real-world applications.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fcac538-3c7c-4864-8057-252f0c60805f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e767aeb2-8850-441c-bea1-7ff98f729db7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ae81e819-6db8-4874-9d70-6cdb4bafa27a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students take out their **Problem Solving with AI Unit Guide.**\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Have students list everything they observe in the image found in the \"What's in an image?\" section of Lesson 2 in their Unit Guides. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students are listing observations, listen for students focusing only on objects rather than details like lighting, time of day, or social context. If needed, ask: \"What details beyond objects help describe this image?\"",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Add Images"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ae81e819-6db8-4874-9d70-6cdb4bafa27a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "731b6c95-5bae-4a7f-ba77-a8fdddf1bf86",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you have noted what you see in the image, let's see how AI describes it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "731b6c95-5bae-4a7f-ba77-a8fdddf1bf86",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a450204-71e9-428f-84af-3b44b63e06d2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- **Level #1:** Students review multimodal concepts\n- **Level #2:** Students add an image and ask AI Chat to describe it. Students should complete the \"What's in an image?\" and \"Privacy and Ethical Concerns\" sections in their Unit Guide.",
        "progression_name": "AI's Image Observation Skills"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a450204-71e9-428f-84af-3b44b63e06d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c8c58b38-dd8f-42ae-b0a7-9737be2ab63c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students noticing unexpected AI-detected details (e.g., time of day, inferred location). If students struggle, ask students, \"Did AI describe something you missed?\" or \"Did AI infer anything beyond what’s in the image?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to compare their observations with AI's response, ask: \"Did AI notice something you didn’t? Why might that be?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c8c58b38-dd8f-42ae-b0a7-9737be2ab63c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0a95d68-d15b-4a55-be96-9aaa91b0b6ed",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0a95d68-d15b-4a55-be96-9aaa91b0b6ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4a7c933b-f320-4eb7-aae7-754a37ee3dd2",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts:\n- *What should we consider before sharing images online with AI tools?*\n- *What are the benefits and risks of AI interpreting images without human context?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that AI can infer more than just visible objects in an image, including time, location, and personal details. They should critically evaluate privacy risks and ethical concerns when sharing images with AI.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For responses related to ethical considerations - If students give general answers, push for specificity by asking: \"What’s one real-world example of when an AI-generated image could cause harm?\"\n\nFor responses related to privacy concerns - If students don’t connect privacy risks to real-world consequences, ask: \"How could an AI misinterpret an image and make incorrect assumptions?\"\n\nIf time allows, prompt the class with: \"Based on what was shared, do you think AI should be allowed to process certain types of images? Why or why not?\" to get students thinking about ethics of AI tool use.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4a7c933b-f320-4eb7-aae7-754a37ee3dd2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ca8035d-d8c9-4134-a166-be36c1781883",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Share this example of AI models increasingly having reverse image search capabilities. Providing an image of a location into newer AI models will often output a very accurate guess as to the image's location using only context clues present in the image.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This YouTube Short (https://youtube.com/shorts/qCIzSx0QGVI?si=oKNSi7LlAPgQNFlp) shows how a human uses context clues from an image to find the location within a music video. It illustrates some of the ways in which context is used to pinpoint locations. This article from Tech Crunch also details the debate about privacy and ethics that is arising from this new AI ability: https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/17/the-latest-viral-chatgpt-trend-is-doing-reverse-location-search-from-photos/"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ca8035d-d8c9-4134-a166-be36c1781883",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2ddc4742-c26b-495f-9832-4b50714803b1",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**  Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 3. Students prompt the chatbot to suggest improvements, first based off of a text-only description and then based on an image.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Scenario-Based Multimodal Prompting",
        "progression_name": "Scenario-Based Prompting"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2ddc4742-c26b-495f-9832-4b50714803b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a35e3028-8717-4e70-8e12-f673841a4541",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students who struggle to explain why AI's response changed when using text vs. images. If needed, ask: \"What new details did AI include when you used an image? Was the response more specific or more general?\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a35e3028-8717-4e70-8e12-f673841a4541",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "054c0e4b-88d3-4e33-8f6b-f8e150949194",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 4 to complete the formative assessment.",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "054c0e4b-88d3-4e33-8f6b-f8e150949194",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "607ac3fe-5b24-4581-a83e-2211af63804e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display students' anonymous responses, and choose one of the three approaches below based on how students did:\n\n- **Option 1: Whole-Class Poll & Debrief (Quick Check-In)**\n\n   - Display the poll results for the multiple choice question.\n\n   - Call on 2-3 students from each answer group (Text Only vs Text +Image) to explain their reasoning\n\n   - Wrap up by asking students: “How does this confirm or challenge what we learned about effective prompting?”\n\n- **Option 2: Small Group Discussion & Class Share-Out (Collaborative)**\n\n   - Assign students to groups of 3-4.\n\n   - Each group discusses: How did AI’s response change when you included an image? and What do you think influenced AI’s different responses?\n\n   - Groups write down 1-2 insights on a shared board (physical or digital).\n\n   - Teacher facilitates a brief whole-class discussion based on key themes.\n\n- **Option 3: Silent Gallery Walk (Reflective)**\n\n   - Post anonymized student responses using the “View Student Responses” Pin Response feature.\n\n   - Students independently respond to pinned responses using:\n\n   - Agree/Disagree: Do they agree with the reasoning? Why?\n\n   - Question: What would they ask this student to clarify?\n\n   - Connection: How does this relate to prior learning?\n\n   - End with whole-class synthesis: What patterns or surprises did students notice?\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This level can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of the lesson objectives. Here are things to look for and actions you can take:\n- If most students correctly identified how AI's response changed with an image, reinforce key takeaways with a brief discussion and move forward to the choice-level scenario activity.\n- If responses indicate confusion about why AI responded differently to text vs. image inputs, ask students to revisit AI's descriptions and reflect on what details were added or changed.\n- If students struggle to connect this to effective prompting, review the warm up discussion about media choices and ask: \"How does this activity confirm what we said about text vs. image information?\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "607ac3fe-5b24-4581-a83e-2211af63804e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d88a4c10-7d30-4511-88bf-787d1f25aa2d",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d88a4c10-7d30-4511-88bf-787d1f25aa2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b0b2323-5646-4483-87f5-967a4674c849",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've discussed your findings, let's practice using multimodal AI in a scenario!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b0b2323-5646-4483-87f5-967a4674c849",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ad3c401-71f1-4262-8e78-1ee38269eda0",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**  Direct students to Lesson 2, Levels 5 to pick one of the scenarios to practice prompting the AI for suggestions using images and text.",
        "progression_name": "Practice with a Multimodal Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ad3c401-71f1-4262-8e78-1ee38269eda0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b71eaeae-78fb-426f-a3d7-d0fd9b049dee",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students recognize how AI's response shifts when using text vs. images. If students only describe objects AI listed, ask: \"Did AI provide any deeper insights beyond what you expected?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to experiment with different combinations of text, images, and PDFs to see how AI's responses change. If students finish early or need an extra challenge, suggest trying Option D: Investigative Journalism, where they can analyze how AI generates headlines and summaries from different sources. Have them compare AI-generated headlines with real-world news articles on the same topic. For an added challenge, they can test how changing a single keyword, altering the image, or adding a conflicting source affects AI's response and credibility."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b71eaeae-78fb-426f-a3d7-d0fd9b049dee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ffeaa74e-6dff-4315-b080-38e540efb184",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What key details did AI focus on in the image? When you prompted AI Chat with an image and a PDF, how did its response change?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that AI was able to pull out key details from the images. Adding in a PDF helped give additional context to AI’s response and make it more accurate.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Not all students interacted with an AI Chat level that required them to add a document. As you circulate during students’ work time, find students who added both an image and a PDF to call on during the discussion."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ffeaa74e-6dff-4315-b080-38e540efb184",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23892759-aa92-4e19-a99c-6483e408807e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42c87b5c-6405-4c3d-9923-b2385f4851d6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to their Unit Guides to answer the Question of the Day. Give them 30 seconds to write a response using one of the sentence starters found in the \"Hint' of the Question of the Day for this lesson before having students share with a partner or ask several students to share with the class.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This works well as an exit ticket or a quick way to gauge comprehension during a lesson. If time allows, guide the class discussion toward real-world applications. Use these prompts if needed:\n- Where have you seen multimodal AI used outside of chatbots?\n- How might multimodal AI impact careers in accessibility, medicine, or security?\n- What are examples of AI tools that use images and text together? (e.g., Google Lens, AI-generated captions, AI medical imaging analysis)\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses to gauge understanding. If students struggle to explain how AI's response changed based on input type, revisit earlier examples and prompt students to compare outputs with and without images. If most responses show a strong grasp, move forward."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42c87b5c-6405-4c3d-9923-b2385f4851d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6ac72de3-07cf-4fce-b031-91a5f6bf9db5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88517f58-3c0b-4f07-afbc-1856d079576e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88517f58-3c0b-4f07-afbc-1856d079576e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6ac72de3-07cf-4fce-b031-91a5f6bf9db5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8bc086c4-1ac0-44b3-8734-a2da75ed5141",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8bc086c4-1ac0-44b3-8734-a2da75ed5141",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6ac72de3-07cf-4fce-b031-91a5f6bf9db5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23f9107a-aaf3-4677-a1b5-5b91e4786ef1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask the class for a topic (e.g., space, animals, history, music, etc.).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use Lesson 5, Level 1 to demo entering the following prompt: \"Tell me 2 truths and a lie about [insert topic]. Don't tell me which ones are truths or lies.\"\n",
        "name": "Two Truths and a Lie",
        "progression_name": "Two Truths and a Lie"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23f9107a-aaf3-4677-a1b5-5b91e4786ef1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1aedcba6-ef62-48b1-bc6a-ca9fdb77849e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d50f16c7-2e69-4cd8-a90b-79c8c27a2d08",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students partner up and try to guess which AI-generated statements are true and which is the lie."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d50f16c7-2e69-4cd8-a90b-79c8c27a2d08",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1aedcba6-ef62-48b1-bc6a-ca9fdb77849e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4bfb125f-9ae0-4a3c-a576-b6724681db91",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI might not always produce the results that is expected. The result might even be contradictory. Therefore, fact-checking AI's results is an important step of the process.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4bfb125f-9ae0-4a3c-a576-b6724681db91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1aedcba6-ef62-48b1-bc6a-ca9fdb77849e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0dd2a98f-9233-4cd1-8e4a-783f2528483b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Demonstrate fact-checking by researching online using Google and credible websites to verify which statements are true and which is a lie.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Why might AI generate a lie or inaccurate information?*\n- *How do we ensure accuracy when using AI?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that AI generates responses based on patterns in data rather than verified facts. They should discuss how AI lacks direct knowledge, can misinterpret ambiguous prompts, and sometimes \"hallucinates\" information because it is designed to predict plausible-sounding text rather than confirm truth. Students highlight the importance of human judgement in verifying AI-generated content. Students should identify fact-checking strategies, such as cross-referencing multiple reliable sources (using official or expert-verified information) and critically analyze responses for logical consistency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0dd2a98f-9233-4cd1-8e4a-783f2528483b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1aedcba6-ef62-48b1-bc6a-ca9fdb77849e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3e5f95c-ddd7-4792-be06-7323f5942d37",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *contradiction*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair of students a copy of the Examining AI Contradictions activity guide.\n\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Contradictions in Real-World Contexts",
        "progression_name": "Examining AI Contradictions Activity Guide"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3e5f95c-ddd7-4792-be06-7323f5942d37",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0e9b885d-1f19-4d7e-8a6c-b89152b3e47f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dcd5e8fe-1709-443f-9cb5-5a00c213e8f4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Using their activity guide, students will review two contradictory AI responses from a telehealth chatbot.\n- **Step 1:** Students review both AI-generated responses.\n- **Step 2:** Students highlight contradictions, vague statements, or misleading claims, such as the conflicting advice on whether or not immediate medical attention is necessary.\n- **Step 3:** Students fact-check the AI's claims using reliable sources (e.g., trusted medical websites or professional guidelines).\n- **Step 4:** Students refine the provided prompt to get a more consistent and clearer response from the AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does the AI's lack of specialization in the medical field contribute to contradictions?*\n- *How can fact-checking and prompt refinement help improve AI-generated responses in real-world contexts?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognized that AI models do not have deep expertise like medical professionals. They should discuss how AI relies on broad datasets that may contain conflicting or outdated information, which leads to contradictions. Students should understand that fact-checking ensures accuracy by verifying AI-generated information against trusted sources. They should also recognize that refining prompts (e.g. specifying a reliable source, adding context, etc.) helps AI produce clearer and more relevant responses.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dcd5e8fe-1709-443f-9cb5-5a00c213e8f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0e9b885d-1f19-4d7e-8a6c-b89152b3e47f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "173eb2af-169c-4a2a-9e27-953e82ecfa6a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI models do not have deep expertise like medical professionals. AI relies on broad datasets that may contain conflicting or outdated information, which leads to contradictions. Fact-checking ensures accuracy by verifying AI-generated information against trusted sources. Refining prompts by doing things like specifying a reliable source or adding context helps AI produce clearer and more relevant responses.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "173eb2af-169c-4a2a-9e27-953e82ecfa6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0e9b885d-1f19-4d7e-8a6c-b89152b3e47f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4192be0d-7794-430e-b8b5-15ed9840e9b7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the differences between where an AI and a medical professional acquire information."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4192be0d-7794-430e-b8b5-15ed9840e9b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0e9b885d-1f19-4d7e-8a6c-b89152b3e47f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f556819f-cd2f-4baf-92f7-1b192e7697b7",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 and 4. Students choose a different topic in this choice level (e.g., government, technology, education, social media). In pairs, students practice with the various forms of questioning provided and discuss the results. Have them add their reflections to the last page of their Activity Guide.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "AI Contradictions in Practice",
        "progression_name": "AI Contradictions in Practice"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f556819f-cd2f-4baf-92f7-1b192e7697b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0e9b885d-1f19-4d7e-8a6c-b89152b3e47f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "190dd689-34f1-46d3-b63a-b2edd0971adc",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to review the three different ways in which students were asked to refine the prompt in the previous activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *context*.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "190dd689-34f1-46d3-b63a-b2edd0971adc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0e9b885d-1f19-4d7e-8a6c-b89152b3e47f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bf4709d3-3214-42ca-abde-d56e4c2941a2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 5. In pairs, students practice prompting the AI Chat with different framings of their own questions to see if they can produce AI contradictions and can experience themselves how their framing can alter the response an AI outputs.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students aren’t sure what topic to choose, direct them to choose one of the examples from the level:\n- Mandatory physical education in school\n- Free higher education\n- Use of AI in school assignments\n- The current US voting age\n\nIf students need help with prompting the AI, direct to them to modify the prompt examples from the level:\n- Positive framing\n- Negative framing\n- Positive framing with a reliable source\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bf4709d3-3214-42ca-abde-d56e4c2941a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0e9b885d-1f19-4d7e-8a6c-b89152b3e47f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf46aa21-7b97-4aaf-83df-75f5ccf6977a",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Try Your Own"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf46aa21-7b97-4aaf-83df-75f5ccf6977a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0e9b885d-1f19-4d7e-8a6c-b89152b3e47f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36bc3499-29da-4153-8cd0-18c5a6e1d7e0",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 7 to complete the formative assessment on Levels 7 and 8.\n",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This level can be used as a formative assessment to check student understanding of the lesson objectives. Here are things to look for and actions you can take:\n- Review responses to see whether students can explain why AI might contradict itself and how that differs from human contradictions. If students struggle to articulate these differences, have them revisit the telehealth example (or another real-world scenario) and prompt them to highlight exactly where the AI's contradiction arose—and contrast it with how a human might revise a diagnosis after new data.\n- See whether students can identify specific potential harms, and if not, direct them back to the telehealth chatbot examples provided in the lesson. You could also show short clips or articles about AI's role in other high-stakes fields (finance, insurance) to further underscore the importance of verifying AI outputs.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36bc3499-29da-4153-8cd0-18c5a6e1d7e0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0e9b885d-1f19-4d7e-8a6c-b89152b3e47f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5147983c-2450-458b-8626-5ea1e73c862a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students take out their Problem Solving with AI Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  In their Unit Guide, students answer the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Students make a prediction about how the ability of AI to contradict itself might evolve over the next 10 years.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n",
        "name": "Future Predictions",
        "progression_name": "Student Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5147983c-2450-458b-8626-5ea1e73c862a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20509bd2-4364-4674-bde1-ffd95ab4c122"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28a0ab14-b285-4411-991e-70c9e4a8d2b4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students understand the importance of keeping a human in the loop and where AI might lack creativity or insight.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to take out their Problem Solving with AI Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the \"AI Essay Critique\" section in their Unit Guide to read the paragraph. Explain that the paragraph was generated by an AI using the basic prompt: \"Write a personal essay\". Direct students to identify what the AI did well and where a human might add more value to the writing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What does this introduction do well?*\n- *What is it lacking?*\n- *How could a human collaborating with AI improve this draft?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that AI produces technically correct and well-structured writing. Students also note that AI writing can lack originality, emotion, and personal voice. They suggest ideas about collaborating with AI to \"fine-tune\" the response, or to improving their prompts to provide more context for the original response. This activity lays the groundwork for today’s project. Students will later collaborate with AI themselves to generate and refine creative content, so this is a chance to start thinking critically about how AI performs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  *If AI struggles with originality in personal essays, how might that apply to creative fields like marketing, advertising, and branding?*\n- Stand and Share: students stand and form small groups of 3-4 students from different tables or rows. After discussing the prompt, each group picks one representative to share their ideas with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students make connections between AI’s limitations in personal essays to similar creative challenges in other fields. They understand that creative ideas in marketing and branding will require emotional connections, cultural relevance, and distinct voices that AI will need human input on to be truly effective.",
        "name": "AI Essay Critique",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, prompt them with: \"Would you trust AI to write a Super Bowl ad? Why or why not?\" or \"What makes a good slogan or ad? Could AI come up with it on its own?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28a0ab14-b285-4411-991e-70c9e4a8d2b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3a09ff6-7452-47c6-8246-5ee9a97e16e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bef6f57e-fd69-4b40-8ba5-57a788f3e8c6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bef6f57e-fd69-4b40-8ba5-57a788f3e8c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3a09ff6-7452-47c6-8246-5ee9a97e16e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62788c89-eaf5-493a-802e-e2aff9323be1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've seen how AI can help generate ideas, but also where it might fall short, let’s dive into how we can collaborate with AI to create something truly innovative—where humans and AI can work together to refine and enhance creative projects.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62788c89-eaf5-493a-802e-e2aff9323be1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3a09ff6-7452-47c6-8246-5ee9a97e16e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "091d3d1e-ee6d-4cab-afb2-9afa87f39f0a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "091d3d1e-ee6d-4cab-afb2-9afa87f39f0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3a09ff6-7452-47c6-8246-5ee9a97e16e0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95555080-d02f-4730-8c7e-2811b85273fe",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** In this activity, students will work in small groups to design and launch a new product or app intended to help students with school-related tasks. They will collaborate with AI at each stage of the development process, refining ideas and generating content.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Introduction to AI Collaboration"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95555080-d02f-4730-8c7e-2811b85273fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b77f1b0-25e7-4111-a1f1-39029ed5b66e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9a26dde3-69f3-417b-b881-b3261c23b016",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *More and more music and movie producers, graphic designers, writers and other creatives are taking advantage of collaborative opportunities with AI. How might AI help you in one of these careers?*\n- *Where might AI fall short?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify tasks like generating ideas or structuring content as areas in which AI can help. They mention limitations of AI like lack of personal voice and lack of emotion. They begin to consider ways in which AI can be a collaborator in creative endeavors.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Place students in groups of four.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Collaborate on a Product Launch activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *co-creating*.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Behind the Curtain**: AI tools like ChatGPT and image generators don't really create from scratch, in a traditional sense. Instead, they remix ideas they've encountered before as data. That is why the prompt that we use is so important. A carefully created prompt tells the AI model what to remix, how to do it, and in what voice or style."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9a26dde3-69f3-417b-b881-b3261c23b016",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b77f1b0-25e7-4111-a1f1-39029ed5b66e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c331ebbd-19a7-4de7-a685-3e317c4397a0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to introduce the Product Launch activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct groups to choose a product or app concept designed to help students in school.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct each group member to choose a role and record their choice in their activity guide. The career choices are: copywriter, graphic designer, video director, sound designer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c331ebbd-19a7-4de7-a685-3e317c4397a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b77f1b0-25e7-4111-a1f1-39029ed5b66e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5962659-2e60-4e32-9f90-42aebb49b4eb",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to list out the specific tasks their role involves. Then, have them sort each task into Best for AI, Best for Humans, or Best for Collaboration, discussing their reasoning with a partner or small group."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5962659-2e60-4e32-9f90-42aebb49b4eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b77f1b0-25e7-4111-a1f1-39029ed5b66e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27f27a5d-0283-400e-902d-544da27f679a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Give students hands-on experience using AI to co-create part of a product launch. Students will work through a sequence of AI-powered levels in order to build and refine their content.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Collaborate on a Product Launch"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27f27a5d-0283-400e-902d-544da27f679a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b77f1b0-25e7-4111-a1f1-39029ed5b66e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c00e075e-5782-48db-a2b4-dee16e3ea15f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've seen how creators break tasks into smaller steps. Now you'll use AI to help complete your part of the product launch, just like a real creative team might. Move through the four stages—research, brainstorming, content creation, and refinement.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c00e075e-5782-48db-a2b4-dee16e3ea15f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b77f1b0-25e7-4111-a1f1-39029ed5b66e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6bf4d130-b0cd-4f51-9bb7-b0640c13cf16",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**  Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 1. Students should complete each choice level based on the part of the product they are launching.",
        "progression_name": "Collaborate on a Product Launch"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6bf4d130-b0cd-4f51-9bb7-b0640c13cf16",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b77f1b0-25e7-4111-a1f1-39029ed5b66e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13812532-5a88-45c6-807e-51c0338e0ca2",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are progressing through each stage. Listen for how they talk about AI responses and how they’re improving or combining ideas. Encourage specificity in their reflection and remind them that they are the creative lead and that the AI is here to support, not replace, their ideas."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13812532-5a88-45c6-807e-51c0338e0ca2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b77f1b0-25e7-4111-a1f1-39029ed5b66e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3d27f28-2d0a-4b9b-bc0d-df3a740eba64",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you've completed your part of the campaign with AI, let's reflect on the collaboration. Think about what you gained from using AI and what could have been lost if you relied only on it. These questions help us understand AI's strengths and limitations in creative work.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3d27f28-2d0a-4b9b-bc0d-df3a740eba64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b77f1b0-25e7-4111-a1f1-39029ed5b66e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34d268d5-8292-487c-84dc-d1dcf36bcfdf",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 6 to complete the formative assessment.",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students only focus on the benefits of AI (e.g., saving time, generating ideas), prompt them with: \"What would have been lost if AI had handled everything on its own? What creative elements did you contribute that AI couldn't replicate?\" This encourages them to think about AI as a complement, not a replacement, for human creativity."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This assessment will help you gauge how well students understand the role of AI as a collaborator. Pay attention to the following:\n\n- Balanced responses: If students acknowledge both the benefits of AI and the essential human contributions, they understand how AI can enhance creativity without replacing it.\n\n- Specific examples: Are students providing detailed answers based on their roles in the campaign (e.g., how AI helped generate slogans or ideas, and what human creativity brought to the project)?\n\n- Critical thinking: Are students able to identify specific aspects of their work that AI couldn't handle, such as adding emotional depth, personal perspective, or fine-tuning content?\n\n**Actions to take:**\n\n- If students focus too much on AI’s benefits (e.g., efficiency, idea generation), remind them of the unique human elements that make creative work impactful, like originality, emotion, and context. Revisit the \"AI Essay Critique\" activity from earlier in the lesson to highlight these aspects.\n\n- If students struggle to justify their reasoning or give vague answers, prompt them with: \"Can you explain how AI's suggestions were useful and where human input was essential?\" This will guide students to connect their reflections to the different stages in the \"Collaborate on a Product Launch\" activity (e.g., brainstorming, refining).\n\n- If students demonstrate strong understanding, celebrate their insights by encouraging them to share with the class. They should be able to explain how AI and human contributions work together to produce a final, polished creative piece.\n\n**Next Step:**\n\n- If most students are able to reflect critically and balance the strengths and weaknesses of AI, continue with the wrap-up discussion to consolidate their learning.\n\n- If some students need more support, consider having a follow-up conversation or provide extra examples to reinforce AI’s complementary role in creative work."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34d268d5-8292-487c-84dc-d1dcf36bcfdf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1b77f1b0-25e7-4111-a1f1-39029ed5b66e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3505b4d8-5ef5-4efd-9300-40c480fae8ba",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** To encourage students to reflect on how AI enhanced the creativity of their group's product launch project and express their thoughts through discussion.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to \"Stand and Swap.\" First, students stand and discuss the Question of the Day within their original project group. Then, each group \"swaps\" by joining with another group nearby to share insights. Groups compare experiences and reflect on how AI helped shape or improve their creative process.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Student Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3505b4d8-5ef5-4efd-9300-40c480fae8ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "244fc029-5820-487e-b721-5ac3b5e23fa0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1465d725-87f6-4b51-8e59-c75117eda681",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\nWhat's a product you use for your hobby or interest that clearly wasn't designed for someone like you?\nWhat would the maker need to know about you to fix it?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students surface the gap between generic products and specific users — the exact gap their own product will fill. Listen for answers that name concrete frustrations (\"the sizing runs huge,\" \"the app assumes you already know the rules\") rather than vague dislikes. Use these to point forward: today students will define a product for an audience they actually know, with the specificity of \"freshman wide receivers on our team,\" not \"athletes.\"\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm-Up",
        "progression_name": "Define & Build",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, prompt them to consider what evidence would be needed to make a prediction believable. "
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1465d725-87f6-4b51-8e59-c75117eda681",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c267aec3-8c0f-48e4-9e0b-e2e7c8c64cbb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5f3c120-6595-42b8-a109-b11c547a7dce",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "####Project Overview & Preparation  (10 min)\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Introduce students to the Product Launch project, its goals, and expected deliverables. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Organize students into their project teams using whatever team size works for your class. Explain that everyone shares one product, one spec sheet, and one chatbot — but each team member will own a different launch job on Day 3, so every member produces individual work the team depends on.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Teams where members know the same hobby or interest will define faster, but mixed-interest teams often write sharper spec sheets because they're forced to explain the basics. Either works — just make sure the chosen topic is something at least one member knows personally, since the rubric rewards a \"specific, personally-known problem.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5f3c120-6595-42b8-a109-b11c547a7dce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c267aec3-8c0f-48e4-9e0b-e2e7c8c64cbb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "452bb1c2-3d06-4ccd-b561-39d8045b0a27",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Introduce students to the Product Launch project, its goals, and expected deliverables. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Product Launch Project Planning Guide. Explain that the guide walks them through the whole project, but their actual work will live in Sketch Lab and AI Chat levels — the guide is their map, not their deliverable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that in this project, students will be working with AI to invent a fictional product that solves a real problem from their own life, define exactly who it's for, and then launch it— with a factual spec sheet, a chatbot for potential customers that students build and test themselves, and a social media post that introduces their product to the world.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "452bb1c2-3d06-4ccd-b561-39d8045b0a27",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c267aec3-8c0f-48e4-9e0b-e2e7c8c64cbb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5240fff5-2363-4e76-83ac-7b52efe9f8ac",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to review the assessment criteria rubric and final deliverables on the last pages of the Project Planning Guide. Explain the design challenge framing of \"How can we use AI as a creative partner to in order to launch a useful or novel product\". Students should complete the Rubric Reflection section in the planning guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to think about how AI can be a useful tool for co-creation, but one that requires human input and judgment to function effectively. Students often skim rubrics and anchor on the final product row. Point them to the Chatbot Creation row specifically — the requirement to document revisions in response to testing is the part teams most often miss."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5240fff5-2363-4e76-83ac-7b52efe9f8ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c267aec3-8c0f-48e4-9e0b-e2e7c8c64cbb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d26388d5-bd2c-42f7-b733-582722d4695a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* _Which part of the rubric do you feel most confident about?_\n* _What questions do you have about what is expected?_\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify where the rubric asks for evidence of process — testing, revising, keeping/modifying/rejecting AI output — not just finished artifacts. Listen for confusion about \"honest limits (what the product does not do)\"; naming that expectation now sets up today's spec sheet work. Answer expectation questions directly; this is the moment to calibrate the class before planning begins.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d26388d5-bd2c-42f7-b733-582722d4695a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c267aec3-8c0f-48e4-9e0b-e2e7c8c64cbb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c47e084b-cd5b-4f5a-b523-512b0ec2cf51",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the four stages briefly. Define the problem and audience, Build the product's official record and its customer-facing chatbot, Test the chatbot against real questions, and Ship it to the world with a launch post. Emphasize that students use AI at every stage, but they are the decision-makers. The rubric rewards shaping and rejecting AI output, not just accepting it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c47e084b-cd5b-4f5a-b523-512b0ec2cf51",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c267aec3-8c0f-48e4-9e0b-e2e7c8c64cbb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3bad6226-b96c-456a-a10b-d49f66ca706a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review today's agenda. Students move through the Define section of the canvas, then start the Build section — the spec sheet and a first draft of the system prompt. Explain the through-line: everything the chatbot will be able to say on Day 2 comes from what gets written on the spec sheet today. A vague spec sheet produces a chatbot that makes things up.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Today is deliberately front-loaded. Teams don't need a polished system prompt by the bell — a complete spec sheet plus a rough TaCoFoRT draft is a successful Day 1. The prompt gets built into a working chatbot and revised on Day 2."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3bad6226-b96c-456a-a10b-d49f66ca706a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c267aec3-8c0f-48e4-9e0b-e2e7c8c64cbb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f7e6233-4116-492e-9032-786dc03bcd54",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "####Define & Build  (25 min)\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students translate a personally-known problem into a factual product spec. The goal is concrete, honest documentation for a specific audience.\n\n**Say**: Now that you understand the project's structure, let's begin by heading to Sketch Lab and taking the first steps towards launching an amazing product.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1. In the Define section, teams pick a topic they know personally, name three issues, choose one for their product to solve, and identify a specific audience. In the Build section, they complete the Product Spec Sheet — choosing four question categories plus additional \"What it is\" and \"What it is NOT\" requirements. Teams then draft all five TaCoFoRT components which will be used to create their Product Chatbot on Day 2.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As teams work, push for specificity and factual claims. Ask:\n* \"Who exactly is this for? Can you name real people who'd use it?\"\n* \"Is that spec sheet entry a fact about the product, or a hope for it?\"\n* \"What did you write for 'What it is NOT'? What will your chatbot need to decline?\"\n* \"Which spec sheet category was hardest to answer with a concrete detail?\"\n* \"How does your Retrieval component point the chatbot back at your spec sheet?\"\n\nBefore teams move on, confirm the spec sheet and product definition describe the same product — inconsistency here is the top source of chatbot errors on Day 2.\n",
        "progression_name": "Define & Build"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f7e6233-4116-492e-9032-786dc03bcd54",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c267aec3-8c0f-48e4-9e0b-e2e7c8c64cbb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "312bf2d8-ee52-435b-bd62-38999eec29bf",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Level 2 to complete the Day One Reflection. Students write one customer question their chatbot should decline because of what their product does not do, and identify the spec sheet category that was hardest to answer with facts — including what they changed about the product to make it answerable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for decline questions that are genuinely plausible customer questions, not generic ones. Ask: \"Would a real customer actually ask that?\"\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap-Up",
        "progression_name": "Day One Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Optionally, you can have students record their answers to these questions on a 3x5 card to be handed in as an Exit Ticket as they leave the class. "
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "312bf2d8-ee52-435b-bd62-38999eec29bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c267aec3-8c0f-48e4-9e0b-e2e7c8c64cbb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8bac9d6-e59b-4c1b-aeab-f15ac9dc5299",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Think of a time a person or a chatbot gave you a confident answer that turned out to be wrong. How did you find out?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect confident-but-wrong answers to the \"What it is NOT\" entries they wrote yesterday. Listen for the insight that a good chatbot's value comes as much from what it declines as from what it answers. This frames today's test plan directly: one question the chatbot should get correct, one it can't answer from the spec sheet, and one it should refuse.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm-Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8bac9d6-e59b-4c1b-aeab-f15ac9dc5299",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4cb2712-b371-4508-98da-1bdc7206bde5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b175ca0d-26c6-4323-ad7e-09fb6a2b0cfd",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review today's agenda. Students build the chatbot from yesterday's drafted system prompt, then run it through a deliberate test-and-refine cycle. Explain that testing here isn't a formality — the rubric requires documented revisions that respond to specific problems observed in testing, so teams should expect (and want) their first version to fail somewhere.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Teams whose system prompt draft is unfinished should timebox it: get a workable version into AI Chat within the first 10 minutes. A running chatbot they can test beats a perfect prompt they can't. "
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b175ca0d-26c6-4323-ad7e-09fb6a2b0cfd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4cb2712-b371-4508-98da-1bdc7206bde5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e408ca6-7b9b-4241-affb-d40f0772b1d6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "####Build & Test (35 min)\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build, test against realistic questions, diagnose which TaCoFoRT component permitted an error, revise, and re-test. The goal is a documented Problem → Fix cycle grounded in the chatbot's output.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 3. \n* In Level 3a (AI Chat), teams compose their system prompt and create their Product Chatbot. \n* In Level 3b (Sketch Lab), they draft three test questions — one the chatbot should answer fully and accurately, one about a detail the spec sheet does not cover, and one the chatbot should decline — then record what the chatbot actually said, word for word, and document at least one Problem → Refine cycle \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As teams test, check that they're recording real outputs and tracing errors to a component. Ask:\n* \"What exactly did the chatbot say — did you copy its actual words into the canvas?\"\n* \"Did it invent an answer to your question two, or admit the spec sheet doesn't cover it?\"\n* \"Did it decline question three, or try to be helpful anyway?\"\n* \"Which TaCoFoRT component let that error through — Task, Context, Format, Retrieval, or Tone?\"\n* \"After your fix, did you re-ask the same question to confirm the revision worked?\"\n\nBefore teams move on, confirm they've completed at least one full test → revise → re-test cycle with the problem and fix written down.\n",
        "progression_name": "Build & Test",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students will want to fix errors by rewriting the whole prompt. Slow that down: the diagnostic skill is naming which component permitted the error and revising that one. If the chatbot hallucinated a price, the conversation is about Retrieval, not Tone."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e408ca6-7b9b-4241-affb-d40f0772b1d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4cb2712-b371-4508-98da-1bdc7206bde5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3ff637f-1ac1-44ca-a19f-734e597746a7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 4 to complete the Day Two Reflection. Students quote one answer their chatbot invented and name the system prompt component that permitted the error and report what happened when they asked the question their chatbot was supposed to decline.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that quotes are verbatim from the canvas, not paraphrased from memory. Ask:\n* \"Is that the chatbot's actual wording? Pull it from your Test section.\"\n* \"You changed Retrieval — what specifically did the new version say that the old one didn't?\"\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap-Up",
        "progression_name": "Day Two Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite one or two teams to share their best Problem → Fix. Hearing that other teams' chatbots also failed — and got better — normalizes the revision cycle before tomorrow's launch day. If a team's chatbot never invented anything, that's worth probing rather than celebrating — usually it means their test questions were too easy. Encourage them to ask a harder question two before writing \"no errors.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3ff637f-1ac1-44ca-a19f-734e597746a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b4cb2712-b371-4508-98da-1bdc7206bde5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5aebeb17-a3d0-444b-9035-b3d7e791a8e9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There is no designated warm-up on this final day to maximize project completion time.",
        "duration": "0",
        "name": "Warm-Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5aebeb17-a3d0-444b-9035-b3d7e791a8e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "88fad595-6657-464b-90af-7a0dff3afcbe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7dabe5d7-c432-4520-849b-3c8c64126181",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review today's agenda. Teams divide launch work by choosing jobs, produce assets with AI assistance, and assemble everything into the final social media post. Name the two mandatory jobs — Art Director and Copywriter — and note that Sound Designer, Commercial Director, and Community Manager are optional third picks. Emphasize the standard that carries the whole day: every claim in every asset must be a fact the spec sheet can back up. The post launches the product they actually defined, not the one they wish they'd built.",
        "duration": "40",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The Keep / Modify / Reject log is where the rubric's AI Content Creation evidence lives. Remind teams that \"Reject, because it promised a feature we don't have\" is exactly the kind of entry that scores — a first-draft AI asset pasted straight in is what the rubric calls out as Limited."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7dabe5d7-c432-4520-849b-3c8c64126181",
        "lesson_activity.key": "88fad595-6657-464b-90af-7a0dff3afcbe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2cb97a34-d576-4592-967e-c1cae51edfe5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "####Ship (40 min)\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students direct AI as a creative production tool. Each job that students can choose generates assets with AI assistance, while students log what they kept, modified, or rejected and why. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 5. Each team member takes a job in the Ship section of the canvas: Art Director generates and checks the main image, Copywriter drafts the name, slogan, and caption, and the team's third job produces a jingle, a commercial script, or predicted comments with a pinned FAQ reply. Every job logs its AI output in the Keep / Modify / Reject table, and the team assembles final assets into the social media post template.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As teams create assets, keep pulling them back to the spec sheet. Ask:\n* \"Does your image match what the spec sheet says — number of parts, colors, appearance?\"\n* \"Read me your caption. Which spec sheet entry backs up each claim?\"\n* \"What did AI give you that you modified or rejected — and what did you write in the log?\"\n* \"Community Manager: did your chatbot handle the questions you predicted, or does the spec sheet need a fix?\"\n* \"Does anything in this post oversell what the product actually does?\"\n\nBefore teams finalize, confirm the post's claims survive a check against the spec sheet — this is the rubric's bar for the Extensive column.\n",
        "progression_name": "Ship",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Time is the constraint today. Have teams get the mandatory jobs (image + copy) to done before polishing the optional third asset. A coherent post with two strong assets beats three half-finished ones."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2cb97a34-d576-4592-967e-c1cae51edfe5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "88fad595-6657-464b-90af-7a0dff3afcbe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f78c031-294a-4c8c-8661-c0e5d992414f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 6 to complete the final project reflection. Students describe one moment where their judgment overrode AI's output, and one place where AI genuinely made the launch better. Encourage them to pull both answers from their Keep / Modify / Reject log.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Day Three Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "These two questions are the meta-evidence for the whole project: question 1 maps to the rubric's \"modified or rejected AI output and explains why,\" and question 2 shows students can credit AI accurately without overclaiming. Read them alongside the Keep / Modify / Reject log — strong answers cite a specific asset and a specific reason. Together with the canvas (spec sheet, test cycles, final post), this completes your evidence across all four rubric rows."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f78c031-294a-4c8c-8661-c0e5d992414f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "88fad595-6657-464b-90af-7a0dff3afcbe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f95848b2-cb2f-406b-872b-7824604957a4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Optionally, you can have students record their answers to these questions on a 3x5 card to be handed in as an Exit Ticket as they leave the class. "
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f95848b2-cb2f-406b-872b-7824604957a4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "88fad595-6657-464b-90af-7a0dff3afcbe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e48e578c-522f-44a0-9705-e3aa88fffe02",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "After completing this project, students should complete the End of Unit Survey linked in the final level. Their responses help us understand their experiences and improve future versions of this unit.",
        "progression_name": "End of Unit Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e48e578c-522f-44a0-9705-e3aa88fffe02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c347dc91-1be3-42cf-8f12-844c8946397e"
      }
    },
